## Memory Persistence Across Sessions

**Diagram: Session Lifecycle**

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant CLI as "claude -p"
    participant SessionStart as "scripts/hooks/session-start.js"
    participant SessionMgr as "scripts/lib/session-manager.js"
    participant Work as "Tool Execution"
    participant SessionEnd as "scripts/hooks/session-end.js"
    participant File as "~/.claude/sessions/<br/>YYYY-MM-DD-shortid-session.tmp"
    
    User->>CLI: Start new session
    CLI->>SessionStart: SessionStart hook
    SessionStart->>SessionMgr: getAllSessions()
    SessionMgr->>File: Read previous session
    File-->>SessionMgr: Session summary markdown
    SessionMgr-->>SessionStart: Context loaded
    SessionStart-->>CLI: Inject context
    
    CLI->>Work: Execute tools
    Work->>Work: Read, Edit, Write, Bash
    
    User->>CLI: End session
    CLI->>SessionEnd: SessionEnd hook
    SessionEnd->>SessionMgr: writeSessionContent()
    SessionMgr->>File: Save state:<br/>- Tasks completed<br/>- Files modified<br/>- Tools used<br/>- Session metadata
```

Session files (`~/.claude/sessions/*.tmp`) provide cross-session memory:

- **SessionStart hook** - Loads previous session's summary into new session context
- **Stop hook** - Runs once per session to extract patterns (not per message to avoid latency)
- **SessionEnd hook** - Persists session summary in markdown format
- **Session aliases** - Human-readable names for long session IDs via `session-aliases.json`

This enables autonomous loops where each `claude -p` invocation accesses historical context through session files, even though the underlying Claude instance has no memory of prior interactions.

**Sources:** [the-longform-guide.md:38-86](), [README.md:348-354](), Diagram 5 from context

