# Page: Core Concepts

# Core Concepts

<details>
<summary>Relevant source files</summary>

The following files were used as context for generating this wiki page:

- [README.md](README.md)
- [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md](skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md)
- [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md](skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md)
- [the-longform-guide.md](the-longform-guide.md)
- [the-shortform-guide.md](the-shortform-guide.md)

</details>



This document introduces the foundational architecture and component types of the Everything Claude Code plugin. It provides a conceptual overview of how agents, skills, commands, rules, hooks, and MCPs work together to create a multi-layered development workflow system.

For detailed information about specific component types, see the following pages:
- Plugin Architecture: [#3.1](#3.1)
- Agents: [#3.2](#3.2)
- Skills: [#3.3](#3.3)
- Commands: [#3.4](#3.4)
- Rules: [#3.5](#3.5)
- Hooks: [#3.6](#3.6)
- MCPs: [#3.7](#3.7)

For installation and configuration details, see [Installation & Setup](#4).

---

## Component Types

The plugin provides six primary component types, each serving a distinct role in the development workflow:

| Component | Location | Purpose | Invocation Method |
|-----------|----------|---------|-------------------|
| **Agents** | `agents/*.md` | Specialized subagents with limited tool permissions for delegated tasks | Via command delegation or direct agent invocation |
| **Skills** | `skills/*/` | Domain knowledge and workflow definitions that inform agent behavior | Auto-loaded by context; referenced by agents and commands |
| **Commands** | `commands/*.md` | Slash commands for quick workflow execution | User types `/command-name` |
| **Rules** | `rules/*.md` | Always-follow guidelines that constrain all agent behavior | Auto-loaded in every session |
| **Hooks** | `hooks/hooks.json` + `scripts/hooks/*.js` | Event-driven automation triggered by tool usage and lifecycle events | Automatic on event triggers |
| **MCPs** | `mcp-configs/mcp-servers.json` | External service integrations (GitHub, Supabase, Vercel, etc.) | Tool calls from Claude |

**Sources:** [README.md:139-241](), [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42](), [the-shortform-guide.md:14-95]()

---

## The Layered Architecture

The plugin operates in four distinct architectural layers, each building on the previous:

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph Layer1["User-Facing Layer"]
        Commands["Commands<br/>(commands/*.md)<br/>/plan /tdd /code-review"]
        Rules["Rules<br/>(rules/*.md)<br/>security.md testing.md"]
    end
    
    subgraph Layer2["Intelligence Layer"]
        Agents["Agents<br/>(agents/*.md)<br/>planner architect code-reviewer"]
        Skills["Skills<br/>(skills/*/)<br/>coding-standards/ tdd-workflow/"]
    end
    
    subgraph Layer3["Automation Layer"]
        Hooks["Hooks<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)<br/>PreToolUse PostToolUse Stop"]
        Scripts["Scripts<br/>(scripts/hooks/*.js)<br/>session-start session-end"]
    end
    
    subgraph Layer4["Learning Layer"]
        CLv1["Continuous Learning v1<br/>(skills/continuous-learning/)<br/>evaluate-session.js"]
        CLv2["Continuous Learning v2<br/>(skills/continuous-learning-v2/)<br/>observations.jsonl → instincts"]
    end
    
    Commands -->|"invoke"| Agents
    Agents -->|"reference"| Skills
    Rules -->|"constrain"| Agents
    Hooks -->|"execute"| Scripts
    Scripts -->|"manage"| CLv1
    Scripts -->|"manage"| CLv2
    CLv1 -->|"generate"| Skills
    CLv2 -->|"generate"| Skills
    CLv2 -->|"generate"| Commands
    CLv2 -->|"generate"| Agents
```

**Layer 1: User-Facing** - Direct user interaction through slash commands and enforced guidelines

**Layer 2: Intelligence** - Task execution through specialized agents informed by domain knowledge

**Layer 3: Automation** - Event-driven processes that run automatically during tool usage

**Layer 4: Learning** - Systems that observe patterns and generate new components

**Sources:** [README.md:139-241](), [the-shortform-guide.md:14-37](), [the-longform-guide.md:38-103]()

---

## Component Discovery and Loading

The Claude Code CLI discovers and loads components through the plugin manifest and conventional directory structures:

### Plugin Manifest Structure

```mermaid
graph LR
    PluginJSON[".claude-plugin/plugin.json"]
    AgentPaths["agents array<br/>./agents/planner.md<br/>./agents/architect.md"]
    SkillPaths["skills array<br/>./skills/<br/>./commands/"]
    
    PluginJSON -->|"declares"| AgentPaths
    PluginJSON -->|"declares"| SkillPaths
    
    AgentPaths -->|"loads"| AgentFiles["~/.claude/agents/<br/>or plugin dir"]
    SkillPaths -->|"loads"| SkillDirs["~/.claude/skills/<br/>or plugin dir"]
```

The manifest at [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42]() declares component paths:

- **agents**: Array of specific agent file paths (lines 26-40)
- **skills**: Array of directory paths that may contain skills or commands (line 25)
- **hooks**: Auto-loaded from `hooks/hooks.json` by convention (not declared in manifest)

**Sources:** [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42](), [README.md:296-318]()

---

## File Organization Pattern

The plugin follows a strict directory convention for component organization:

```
everything-claude-code/
├── agents/                    # Specialized subagents
│   ├── planner.md            # Planning agent
│   ├── architect.md          # Architecture decisions
│   ├── tdd-guide.md          # Test-driven development
│   └── code-reviewer.md      # Quality review
├── skills/                    # Domain knowledge
│   ├── coding-standards/     # Language best practices
│   ├── tdd-workflow/         # TDD methodology
│   └── continuous-learning/  # Auto-learning patterns
├── commands/                  # Slash commands
│   ├── plan.md               # /plan command
│   ├── tdd.md                # /tdd command
│   └── code-review.md        # /code-review command
├── rules/                     # Always-follow guidelines
│   ├── security.md           # Security requirements
│   ├── testing.md            # TDD enforcement
│   └── coding-style.md       # Style guidelines
├── hooks/                     # Event-driven automation
│   └── hooks.json            # Hook declarations
├── scripts/                   # Hook implementations
│   ├── hooks/                # Hook scripts
│   │   ├── session-start.js
│   │   └── session-end.js
│   └── lib/                  # Shared utilities
│       └── utils.js          # Cross-platform helpers
└── .claude-plugin/           # Plugin metadata
    └── plugin.json           # Component declarations
```

**Sources:** [README.md:139-241](), [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42]()

---

## Component Interaction Patterns

Components interact through several well-defined patterns:

### Pattern 1: Command → Agent Delegation

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant Command as "Command<br/>(commands/tdd.md)"
    participant Agent as "Agent<br/>(agents/tdd-guide.md)"
    participant Skill as "Skill<br/>(skills/tdd-workflow/)"
    
    User->>Command: "/tdd implement feature"
    Command->>Agent: "Delegate with context"
    Agent->>Skill: "Reference workflow"
    Skill-->>Agent: "TDD methodology"
    Agent-->>Command: "Execute with tools"
    Command-->>User: "Implementation complete"
```

Example: The `/tdd` command at [commands/tdd.md]() invokes the `tdd-guide` agent at [agents/tdd-guide.md](), which references the `tdd-workflow` skill at [skills/tdd-workflow/]().

**Sources:** [README.md:176-190](), [the-shortform-guide.md:76-96]()

---

### Pattern 2: Hook → Script Execution

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant CLI as "Claude Code CLI"
    participant Hook as "Hook Matcher<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)"
    participant Script as "Node.js Script<br/>(scripts/hooks/*.js)"
    participant File as "File System"
    
    CLI->>Hook: "PreToolUse: Edit tool"
    Hook->>Hook: "Match file pattern"
    Hook->>Script: "Execute script"
    Script->>File: "Run prettier --write"
    File-->>Script: "Formatting complete"
    Script-->>CLI: "Exit code 0"
```

Example: PostToolUse hooks at [hooks/hooks.json]() execute formatting scripts when Edit tool is used.

**Sources:** [README.md:205-221](), [the-shortform-guide.md:38-72]()

---

### Pattern 3: Continuous Learning Pipeline

```mermaid
graph LR
    Session["Session Activity"]
    PreHook["PreToolUse Hook<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)"]
    PostHook["PostToolUse Hook<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)"]
    ObsLog["observations.jsonl<br/>(~/.claude/homunculus/)"]
    Observer["Background Observer<br/>(Haiku model)"]
    Instincts["Instincts<br/>(homunculus/instincts/personal/)"]
    Evolve["Evolution Engine<br/>(/evolve command)"]
    Artifacts["Generated Components<br/>(homunculus/evolved/)"]
    
    Session -->|"before tool"| PreHook
    Session -->|"after tool"| PostHook
    PreHook -->|"append"| ObsLog
    PostHook -->|"append"| ObsLog
    ObsLog -->|"periodic read"| Observer
    Observer -->|"creates"| Instincts
    Instincts -->|"cluster 3+"| Evolve
    Evolve -->|"generates"| Artifacts
```

The continuous learning v2 system uses hooks to capture 100% of tool usage, analyzes patterns with a background observer, creates atomic instincts with confidence scores, and clusters them into reusable components.

**Sources:** [README.md:279-291](), [skills/continuous-learning-v2/]()

---

## Component Format Specifications

Each component type follows a specific file format:

### Agent Format

Agents use YAML frontmatter with markdown body:

```markdown
---
name: agent-name
description: Brief description of agent purpose
tools: ["Read", "Write", "Bash"]
model: sonnet | opus | haiku
---

Agent system prompt and instructions...
```

**Sources:** [README.md:407-419](), [agents/planner.md](), [agents/code-reviewer.md]()

---

### Skill Format

Skills use plain markdown with optional subdirectories:

```
skills/
├── skill-name/
│   ├── README.md           # Main skill content
│   ├── patterns.md         # Pattern definitions
│   └── examples.md         # Usage examples
└── simple-skill.md         # Single-file skill
```

**Sources:** [README.md:421-433](), [skills/tdd-workflow/](), [skills/coding-standards/]()

---

### Command Format

Commands use plain markdown with optional agent invocation:

```markdown
# Command Name

Brief description of what this command does.

**Usage:** /command-name [arguments]

**Agent Delegation:**
Invoke the `agent-name` agent with the following context:
- Parameter 1
- Parameter 2

**Workflow:**
1. Step one
2. Step two
```

**Sources:** [commands/tdd.md](), [commands/plan.md](), [commands/code-review.md]()

---

### Hook Format

Hooks use JSON configuration with matcher expressions:

```json
{
  "PreToolUse": [
    {
      "matcher": "tool == \"Edit\" && file_path matches \"\\.ts$\"",
      "hooks": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "node scripts/hooks/check-types.js \"$file_path\""
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Sources:** [hooks/hooks.json](), [README.md:435-447](), [the-shortform-guide.md:40-67]()

---

## Data Flow: User Request to Execution

The complete flow from user input to execution involves multiple component interactions:

```mermaid
graph TB
    UserInput["User Input<br/>/plan 'add auth'"]
    
    CLIParser["CLI Parser"]
    CommandLoader["Command Loader<br/>(commands/plan.md)"]
    
    RuleEngine["Rule Enforcement<br/>(rules/*.md)"]
    
    AgentInvoke["Agent Invocation<br/>(agents/planner.md)"]
    
    SkillLoad["Skill Loading<br/>(skills/*)"]
    
    ToolExec["Tool Execution<br/>(Read, Write, Bash)"]
    
    HookPre["PreToolUse Hooks<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)"]
    HookPost["PostToolUse Hooks<br/>(hooks/hooks.json)"]
    
    ScriptExec["Script Execution<br/>(scripts/hooks/*.js)"]
    
    Response["Response to User"]
    
    UserInput --> CLIParser
    CLIParser --> CommandLoader
    CommandLoader --> RuleEngine
    RuleEngine --> AgentInvoke
    AgentInvoke --> SkillLoad
    SkillLoad --> ToolExec
    ToolExec --> HookPre
    HookPre --> ScriptExec
    ScriptExec --> HookPost
    HookPost --> Response
```

**Sources:** [README.md:139-241](), [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42]()

---

## Component Scope and Boundaries

Components operate within defined scope boundaries to prevent interference:

| Component | Scope | Constraint Mechanism |
|-----------|-------|---------------------|
| **Agents** | Limited by tool permissions and model selection | `tools` array in frontmatter limits available tools |
| **Skills** | Referenced by specific agents or commands | Directory structure and naming convention |
| **Commands** | User-initiated workflows | Explicit invocation via `/command-name` |
| **Rules** | Global enforcement across all agents | Auto-loaded in every session |
| **Hooks** | Specific tool or lifecycle events | Matcher expressions in `hooks.json` |
| **MCPs** | External service boundaries | Individual MCP server configuration |

### Tool Permission Boundaries

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph FullPermissions["Full Permissions"]
        Orchestrator["orchestrator agent<br/>Tools: All"]
        Planner["planner agent<br/>Tools: Read Grep Glob"]
    end
    
    subgraph LimitedPermissions["Limited Permissions"]
        Architect["architect agent<br/>Tools: Read Bash<br/>NO Write/Edit"]
        Reviewer["code-reviewer agent<br/>Tools: Read Grep<br/>NO Write/Edit/Bash"]
    end
    
    subgraph EditPermissions["Edit Permissions"]
        TDDGuide["tdd-guide agent<br/>Tools: Edit Bash Grep"]
    end
    
    Orchestrator -->|"delegates to"| Architect
    Orchestrator -->|"delegates to"| Reviewer
    Planner -->|"delegates to"| Architect
```

Agents with read-only permissions (architect, code-reviewer) cannot modify code, ensuring safe analysis and review workflows.

**Sources:** [agents/planner.md](), [agents/architect.md](), [agents/code-reviewer.md](), [agents/tdd-guide.md]()

---

## Cross-Component Communication

Components communicate through several mechanisms:

### File-Based Handoff Documents

Orchestrator agents use intermediate files to pass context between sequential agent phases:

```
Phase 1: planner agent → generates plan.md
Phase 2: architect agent → reads plan.md → generates architecture.md  
Phase 3: tdd-guide agent → reads architecture.md → implements code
Phase 4: code-reviewer agent → reads code → generates review.md
```

**Sources:** [the-longform-guide.md:269-286](), [agents/planner.md]()

---

### Skill References

Agents explicitly reference skills in their prompts:

```markdown
---
name: tdd-guide
---

You are a test-driven development guide.

**Reference Skills:**
- skills/tdd-workflow/ - Follow the RED-GREEN-REFACTOR cycle
- skills/coding-standards/ - Apply language-specific best practices
```

**Sources:** [agents/tdd-guide.md](), [skills/tdd-workflow/]()

---

### Hook-Triggered State Persistence

Hooks save state to files for cross-session persistence:

- `SessionStart` → loads `~/.claude/sessions/*.tmp` files
- `SessionEnd` → saves current state to `.tmp` files
- `PreCompact` → preserves critical state before compaction

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/session-start.js](), [scripts/hooks/session-end.js](), [scripts/hooks/pre-compact.js]()

---

## Context Window Impact

Different component types have varying impacts on the available context window:

| Component Type | Context Impact | Strategy |
|----------------|----------------|----------|
| Rules | Low (auto-loaded once) | Keep modular, under 10 files |
| Skills | Medium (loaded on reference) | Use lazy loading, selective references |
| Agents | Low (only active agent loaded) | Tool permission scoping reduces context |
| MCPs | **High** (all tools exposed) | Keep <10 enabled, <80 total tools |
| Hooks | Very Low (JSON definitions only) | Scripts execute externally |

The 200k context window can degrade to 70k with excessive MCP enablement. Rule of thumb: 20-30 MCPs configured, but <10 enabled per project.

**Sources:** [README.md:508-520](), [the-shortform-guide.md:137-153](), [the-longform-guide.md:24-33]()

---

## Configuration Discovery Order

The CLI discovers component configurations in a specific priority order:

### Package Manager Detection Priority

1. `CLAUDE_PACKAGE_MANAGER` environment variable
2. `.claude/package-manager.json` (project config)
3. `package.json` `packageManager` field
4. Lock file detection (package-lock.json, yarn.lock, pnpm-lock.yaml, bun.lockb)
5. `~/.claude/package-manager.json` (global config)
6. First available package manager (fallback)

**Sources:** [README.md:106-136](), [scripts/lib/package-manager.js](), [scripts/setup-package-manager.js]()

---

### Component Loading Priority

1. Project-level components (`.claude/`)
2. User-level components (`~/.claude/`)
3. Plugin-provided components (plugin directory)

Project-level configurations override user-level configurations, which override plugin defaults.

**Sources:** [README.md:356-367](), [the-shortform-guide.md:99-123]()

---

## Summary

The Everything Claude Code plugin provides a multi-layered architecture where:

- **Commands** provide user-facing workflow entry points
- **Agents** execute tasks with scoped tool permissions
- **Skills** inform agent behavior with domain knowledge
- **Rules** enforce consistent guidelines across all agents
- **Hooks** automate repetitive tasks through event triggers
- **MCPs** integrate external services

These components work together through file-based handoffs, skill references, and hook-based state management to create a comprehensive development workflow system.

For detailed information about specific component types, see the child pages: [Plugin Architecture](#3.1), [Agents](#3.2), [Skills](#3.3), [Commands](#3.4), [Rules](#3.5), [Hooks](#3.6), and [MCPs](#3.7).

**Sources:** [README.md:1-553](), [.claude-plugin/plugin.json:1-42](), [the-shortform-guide.md:1-431](), [the-longform-guide.md:1-355]()

---

