## Agent File Structure

Agents are Markdown files with YAML frontmatter stored in `~/.claude/agents/` (user-level) or `.claude/agents/` (project-level).

**Diagram: Agent File Anatomy**

```mermaid
graph TB
    AgentFile["agent-name.md"]
    
    subgraph "YAML Frontmatter"
        Name["name: agent-name"]
        Desc["description: Single-line purpose"]
        Tools["tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash]"]
        Model["model: sonnet | opus | haiku"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Markdown Body"
        SystemPrompt["System prompt / instructions<br/>Role definition<br/>Constraints<br/>Output format"]
    end
    
    AgentFile --> Name
    AgentFile --> Desc
    AgentFile --> Tools
    AgentFile --> Model
    AgentFile --> SystemPrompt
```

**Example structure:**

```yaml
---
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for quality, security, and maintainability
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob", "Bash"]
model: opus
---

You are a senior code reviewer specializing in security and maintainability.

Your responsibilities:
1. Check for security vulnerabilities (SQL injection, XSS, etc.)
2. Verify error handling and edge cases
3. Review test coverage
4. Assess code clarity and maintainability

Output format:
- **Issues Found:** List of problems with severity (CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW)
- **Recommendations:** Specific improvements with code examples
- **Verdict:** APPROVED / CHANGES_REQUESTED / BLOCKED
```

**Frontmatter fields:**

| Field | Type | Required | Purpose |
|-------|------|----------|---------|
| `name` | string | Yes | Unique identifier (kebab-case) |
| `description` | string | Yes | Single-line summary (shown in agent lists) |
| `tools` | string[] | Yes | Allowed tool names (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task) |
| `model` | string | No | Model override (haiku, sonnet, opus) — defaults to user selection |

Sources: [README.md:593-609](), [the-shortform-guide.md:389-402]()

---

## Model Configuration

Agents specify which Claude model to use based on task complexity and reasoning depth required.

**Diagram: Model Selection Decision Tree**

```mermaid
graph TD
    Task["Task Requirements"]
    
    Task --> Exploration{"Simple search/<br/>file navigation?"}
    Task --> Implementation{"Multi-file<br/>implementation?"}
    Task --> Architecture{"Architectural<br/>decisions?"}
    Task --> Security{"Security<br/>critical?"}
    Task --> Debugging{"Complex<br/>debugging?"}
    
    Exploration -->|Yes| Haiku["model: haiku<br/>Fast, cheap<br/>$0.25/1M input"]
    Implementation -->|Yes| Sonnet["model: sonnet<br/>Best balance<br/>$3/1M input"]
    Architecture -->|Yes| Opus["model: opus<br/>Deep reasoning<br/>$15/1M input"]
    Security -->|Yes| Opus
    Debugging -->|Complex| Opus
    Debugging -->|Simple| Sonnet
```

**Model selection guidelines:**

| Model | Use Cases | Examples |
|-------|-----------|----------|
| **haiku** | Fast exploration, simple edits, docs | `planner` (initial scan), `doc-updater` |
| **sonnet** | Standard coding, multi-file changes, PR reviews | `tdd-guide`, `build-error-resolver`, `refactor-cleaner` |
| **opus** | Architecture, security, complex bugs | `architect`, `code-reviewer`, `security-reviewer` |

**Escalation pattern:**

```bash
# Start with Sonnet (default)
/code-review

# If first attempt fails or complexity is high, escalate to Opus
/code-review --model opus
```

**Cost reference:**

- **Haiku:** $0.25/1M input, $1.25/1M output
- **Sonnet:** $3/1M input, $15/1M output  
- **Opus:** $15/1M input, $75/1M output

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:104-144](), [README.md:593-609]()

---

## Tool Permissions

Agents declare allowed tools via the `tools` frontmatter field. This creates **security boundaries** and prevents scope creep.

**Available tools:**

| Tool | Capability | Common Usage |
|------|-----------|--------------|
| `Read` | Read file contents | All agents (universal) |
| `Grep` | Search file contents | Code review, search tasks |
| `Glob` | List files matching patterns | Planning, exploration |
| `Write` | Create new files | Implementation agents |
| `Edit` | Modify existing files | Implementation agents |
| `Bash` | Execute shell commands | Build, test, verification |
| `Task` | Delegate to sub-agents | Orchestrators only |

**Diagram: Tool Permission Patterns**

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Read-Only Agents"
        Planner["planner<br/>Read, Grep, Glob"]
        Architect["architect<br/>Read, Grep"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Implementation Agents"
        TDD["tdd-guide<br/>Read, Write, Edit, Bash"]
        BuildResolver["build-error-resolver<br/>Read, Write, Edit, Bash"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Review Agents"
        CodeReviewer["code-reviewer<br/>Read, Grep, Glob, Bash"]
        SecurityReviewer["security-reviewer<br/>Read, Grep, Glob"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Orchestrators"
        ChiefOfStaff["chief-of-staff<br/>Read, Grep, Glob, Task"]
    end
```

**Security principle:** Grant minimum viable tools. A code reviewer should never have `Write` or `Edit` permissions — it prevents author bias (reviewing your own changes).

**Example tool restrictions:**

```yaml
# Planner: read-only exploration
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob"]

# Implementer: full code modification
tools: ["Read", "Write", "Edit", "Bash"]

# Reviewer: read + verification only (no modifications)
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob", "Bash"]
```

Sources: [README.md:593-609](), [the-longform-guide.md:254-286](), [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:451-467]()

