# Page: Skills

# Skills

<details>
<summary>Relevant source files</summary>

The following files were used as context for generating this wiki page:

- [README.md](README.md)
- [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md](skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md)
- [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md](skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md)
- [the-longform-guide.md](the-longform-guide.md)
- [the-shortform-guide.md](the-shortform-guide.md)

</details>



This page defines skills as workflow definitions in the Everything Claude Code system, their markdown structure with YAML frontmatter, and how agents and commands invoke them. For quick-execution prompts, see [Commands](#3.4). For always-follow guidelines, see [Rules](#3.5). For how agents delegate to skills, see [Agents](#3.2).

---

## Overview

Skills are **workflow definitions** that encapsulate reusable execution patterns, domain knowledge, and step-by-step procedures. Unlike rules (always-applied constraints) or commands (single-shot prompts), skills provide detailed guidance for multi-step processes like TDD workflows, security reviews, architectural patterns, or framework-specific best practices.

Skills are stored as markdown files with YAML frontmatter and invoked either directly by agents through delegation or indirectly via slash commands.

**Sources:** [README.md:608-621](), [the-shortform-guide.md:13-36]()

---

## File Structure and Format

### Skill File Anatomy

Every skill follows a standardized markdown structure:

```markdown
---
name: skill-name-kebab-case
description: "Brief description of the workflow or domain knowledge"
origin: ECC | community
---

# Skill Title

Brief overview of what this skill covers and when to use it.

## When to Use

- Specific scenario 1
- Specific scenario 2
- Specific scenario 3

## Workflow Steps / Patterns / Guidelines

Detailed content here...

## Examples (optional)

Concrete examples...

## References (optional)

External links or citations...
```

**Sources:** [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:1-10](), [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:1-10]()

### Directory Organization

Skills are organized in a two-level hierarchy:

```
skills/
├── coding-standards/
│   └── SKILL.md
├── tdd-workflow/
│   └── SKILL.md
├── continuous-learning-v2/
│   └── SKILL.md
├── backend-patterns/
│   └── SKILL.md
└── autonomous-loops/
    └── SKILL.md
```

Each skill lives in its own directory with a `SKILL.md` file as the entry point. Additional supporting files (templates, examples, codemaps) can live in the same directory.

**Sources:** [README.md:233-287](), [the-shortform-guide.md:22-34]()

---

## Frontmatter Schema

### Required Fields

| Field | Type | Purpose | Example |
|-------|------|---------|---------|
| `name` | string | Kebab-case identifier for the skill | `tdd-workflow` |
| `description` | string | One-line summary of the skill's purpose | `"Test-driven development workflow for feature implementation"` |
| `origin` | enum | Source attribution: `ECC` or `community` | `ECC` |

### Example Frontmatter

```yaml
---
name: autonomous-loops
description: "Patterns and architectures for autonomous Claude Code loops — from simple sequential pipelines to RFC-driven multi-agent DAG systems."
origin: ECC
---
```

**Sources:** [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:1-5](), [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:1-5]()

