# Page: Commands

# Commands

<details>
<summary>Relevant source files</summary>

The following files were used as context for generating this wiki page:

- [.claude-plugin/marketplace.json](.claude-plugin/marketplace.json)
- [.claude-plugin/plugin.json](.claude-plugin/plugin.json)
- [README.md](README.md)
- [commands/checkpoint.md](commands/checkpoint.md)
- [commands/eval.md](commands/eval.md)
- [commands/verify.md](commands/verify.md)
- [package.json](package.json)
- [scripts/release.sh](scripts/release.sh)
- [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md](skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md)
- [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md](skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md)
- [the-longform-guide.md](the-longform-guide.md)
- [the-shortform-guide.md](the-shortform-guide.md)

</details>



Commands are slash-invoked prompts that provide quick access to common workflows. They serve as entry points to structured tasks, often delegating to specialized agents or invoking skill workflows.

For information about agents that commands delegate to, see [Agents](#3.2). For details on skill workflows that commands invoke, see [Skills](#3.3). For hook-based automation that runs alongside command execution, see [Hooks](#3.6).

---

## Purpose and Scope

Commands fulfill three primary roles in the ECC system:

1. **Quick Execution Prompts** — Pre-written prompts for common tasks (`/tdd`, `/code-review`, `/plan`)
2. **Agent Delegation Entry Points** — Commands that spawn specialized subagents for focused work
3. **Skill Workflow Triggers** — Commands that invoke multi-step workflows defined in skills

Commands exist in the middle layer of the ECC architecture: users interact with commands, commands orchestrate agents and skills, and hooks enforce quality gates during command execution.

---

## Command Architecture

### File-Based Storage

Commands are stored as markdown files in the `commands/` directory. Each command file defines a prompt template that Claude Code executes when the user invokes the corresponding slash command.

```
commands/
├── tdd.md              # /tdd command
├── plan.md             # /plan command
├── code-review.md      # /code-review command
├── checkpoint.md       # /checkpoint command
├── verify.md           # /verify command
└── eval.md             # /eval command
```

**Sources:** [README.md:289-321](), [package.json:56]()

### Command Structure

Commands follow a simple markdown format with optional YAML frontmatter:

```markdown
---
name: command-name
description: Brief description
---

# Command Title

Detailed instructions for Claude Code to execute.

## Usage

/command [arguments]

## Instructions

Step-by-step execution logic...
```

Unlike agents (which require `model`, `tools`, `name` frontmatter) or skills (which require `name`, `description`, `origin` frontmatter), commands have minimal metadata requirements. The command name is derived from the filename (`tdd.md` → `/tdd`).

**Sources:** [commands/tdd.md:1-74](), [commands/plan.md:1-92](), [commands/checkpoint.md:1-75]()

---

## Command Execution Flow

### Basic Invocation

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant ClaudeCode as "Claude Code CLI"
    participant CmdFile as "commands/verify.md"
    participant Agent as "build-error-resolver<br/>(if needed)"
    participant Hooks as "PostToolUse Hooks"

    User->>ClaudeCode: /verify full
    ClaudeCode->>CmdFile: Read command template
    CmdFile-->>ClaudeCode: Verification instructions
    ClaudeCode->>ClaudeCode: Parse $ARGUMENTS = "full"
    ClaudeCode->>ClaudeCode: Execute instructions
    ClaudeCode->>Hooks: Tool use events (Bash, Read)
    ClaudeCode->>Agent: Delegate if build fails
    Agent-->>ClaudeCode: Return fixes
    ClaudeCode-->>User: Verification report
```

**Sources:** [commands/verify.md:1-60](), [agents/build-error-resolver.md]()

### Argument Parsing

Commands access user-provided arguments via the `$ARGUMENTS` variable. The command template specifies how to parse and interpret these arguments.

**Example:** `/checkpoint create "feature-start"`
- `$ARGUMENTS` = `create "feature-start"`
- Command template parses action (`create`) and name (`"feature-start"`)

**Example:** `/verify quick`
- `$ARGUMENTS` = `quick`
- Command template selects quick verification mode (build + types only)

**Sources:** [commands/checkpoint.md:68-74](), [commands/verify.md:54-59]()

---

## Command Categories

ECC organizes commands into functional categories based on their primary purpose:

### Development Workflow Commands

| Command | Purpose | Primary Agent | Typical Invocation |
