# Page: Rules

# Rules

<details>
<summary>Relevant source files</summary>

The following files were used as context for generating this wiki page:

- [README.md](README.md)
- [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md](skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md)
- [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md](skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md)
- [the-longform-guide.md](the-longform-guide.md)
- [the-shortform-guide.md](the-shortform-guide.md)

</details>



Rules are always-follow guidelines that constrain Claude Code's behavior across all sessions. They form a declarative specification layer that operates above individual prompts, enforcing project-specific patterns, coding standards, security requirements, and workflow conventions without requiring explicit reminders in every interaction.

For detailed listings of available rules and their contents, see [Rules Reference](#5.4). For information about dynamic system prompt injection and context management techniques, see [Memory & Context Preservation](#11.3).

---

## What Rules Are

Rules are Markdown files stored in the `rules/` directory that Claude Code loads at the start of every session. Unlike skills (which are invoked explicitly) or commands (which run on demand), rules are **always active** — they establish the baseline constraints and preferences that guide all agent behavior.

**Core Characteristics:**

| Property | Behavior |
|----------|----------|
| **Scope** | Universal — applies to all operations in a session |
| **Invocation** | Automatic — loaded at session start without user action |
| **Priority** | Higher authority than user messages, lower than system prompts |
| **Persistence** | Session-scoped — reloaded for each new `claude` invocation |
| **Mutability** | Read-only — agents cannot modify rule files (but see [Config Protection](#config-protection)) |

**Example rule statements:**

```markdown
# From coding-style.md
- Prefer immutability: const > let, never use var
- File size limit: 300 lines per file (exceptions: config files)
- No emojis in production code

# From testing.md
- Minimum 80% code coverage for new code
- Write tests before implementation (TDD)
- Test file naming: *.test.ts or *.spec.ts

# From security.md
- No hardcoded secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords)
- Validate all external inputs
- Use parameterized queries for database operations
```

Sources: [README.md:323-336](), [README.md:636-649](), [the-shortform-guide.md:99-123]()

---

## Hierarchical Structure

Rules are organized into a two-tier hierarchy: **common rules** (language-agnostic) and **language-specific rules** (ecosystem conventions).

### Directory Organization

```
rules/
├── README.md                 # Installation guide and structure overview
├── common/                   # Universal principles (always install)
│   ├── coding-style.md       # Immutability, file organization, naming
│   ├── git-workflow.md       # Commit format, branch naming, PR process
│   ├── testing.md            # TDD workflow, coverage requirements
│   ├── performance.md        # Model selection (Haiku/Sonnet/Opus)
│   ├── patterns.md           # Design patterns, architectural principles
│   ├── hooks.md              # Hook usage, TodoWrite pattern
│   ├── agents.md             # When to delegate to subagents
│   └── security.md           # Mandatory security checks
│
├── typescript/               # TypeScript/JavaScript ecosystem
│   ├── style.md              # TS-specific conventions (types, async/await)
│   ├── tooling.md            # Biome, Prettier, ESLint configurations
│   └── frameworks.md         # React, Next.js, Node.js patterns
│
├── python/                   # Python ecosystem
│   ├── style.md              # PEP 8, type hints, docstrings
│   ├── tooling.md            # Ruff, Black, mypy configurations
│   └── frameworks.md         # Django, FastAPI patterns
│
└── golang/                   # Go ecosystem
    ├── style.md              # Go idioms, error handling
    ├── tooling.md            # go fmt, golangci-lint
    └── frameworks.md         # gRPC, Echo patterns
```

**Design Rationale:**

- **Common rules** establish universal principles (security, testing, git workflow) that apply regardless of language
- **Language-specific rules** layer on ecosystem conventions that would conflict across languages (e.g., TypeScript's `const` vs Python's `UPPER_CASE` for constants)
- This prevents rule bloat: a Python project doesn't load TypeScript tooling rules

Sources: [README.md:323-336](), [README.md:639-646]()

---

## Installation Locations

Rules can be installed at two scopes, with different precedence and use cases:

```mermaid
graph TB
    UserRules["~/.claude/rules/<br/>(User-Level)"]
    ProjectRules[".claude/rules/<br/>(Project-Level)"]
    Session["Claude Code Session"]
    
    UserRules -->|"Loaded first<br/>(lower precedence)"| Session
    ProjectRules -->|"Loaded second<br/>(higher precedence)"| Session
    
    UserRules -.->|"Applies to all projects"| Use1["Personal preferences<br/>Common patterns<br/>Security baseline"]
    ProjectRules -.->|"Applies to current project only"| Use2["Project-specific<br/>Team conventions<br/>Framework rules"]
```

**Installation Locations Diagram**

### User-Level Rules (`~/.claude/rules/`)

**Purpose:** Personal preferences and universal standards that apply to all projects.

**Installation:**
```bash
# Copy common rules (always recommended)
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/common/* ~/.claude/rules/

# Add language-specific rules for your primary stack
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/typescript/* ~/.claude/rules/
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/python/* ~/.claude/rules/
```

**Use Cases:**
- Security requirements (no hardcoded secrets, input validation)
- Git workflow conventions (commit message format, branch naming)
- Testing standards (TDD, minimum coverage)
- Personal coding style preferences

### Project-Level Rules (`.claude/rules/`)

**Purpose:** Project-specific conventions and team standards that override user-level rules.

**Installation:**
```bash
# In project root
mkdir -p .claude/rules
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/common/* .claude/rules/
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/typescript/* .claude/rules/
```

**Use Cases:**
- Team-specific style guides (different from personal preferences)
- Framework-specific patterns (Django ORM, Next.js App Router)
- Project-specific constraints (file structure, naming schemes)
- Client requirements or compliance standards

### Precedence Rules

When rules conflict, Claude Code uses this resolution order:

| Priority | Source | Example |
|----------|--------|---------|
| 1 (Highest) | System prompts via CLI flags | `claude --system-prompt "$(cat contexts/dev.md)"` |
| 2 | Project-level rules | `.claude/rules/coding-style.md` |
| 3 | User-level rules | `~/.claude/rules/coding-style.md` |
| 4 (Lowest) | User messages | Direct prompts in the session |

**Practical Example:**

```markdown
# User-level: ~/.claude/rules/testing.md
- Minimum 80% code coverage

# Project-level: .claude/rules/testing.md
- Minimum 95% code coverage (financial compliance requirement)

# Result: 95% coverage enforced (project overrides user)
```

Sources: [README.md:527-544](), [README.md:559-577](), [the-longform-guide.md:38-76]()

---

## How Rules Constrain Behavior

Rules operate as soft constraints through context injection. They are not hardcoded logic but natural language specifications that Claude interprets when making decisions.

### Loading Mechanism

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant CLI as "claude CLI"
    participant Loader as "Rule Loader"
    participant Agent as "Claude Agent"
    
    User->>CLI: claude (start session)
    CLI->>Loader: Load rules
    
    Loader->>Loader: Read ~/.claude/rules/*.md
    Loader->>Loader: Read .claude/rules/*.md
    Loader->>Loader: Concatenate (project overrides user)
    
    Loader->>Agent: Inject as context
    Note over Agent: Rules prepended to context<br/>before user messages
    
    User->>Agent: "Add authentication"
    Agent->>Agent: Check rules:<br/>- security.md (validate inputs)<br/>- testing.md (write tests first)<br/>- git-workflow.md (conventional commits)
    Agent->>User: Implementation with constraints applied
```

**Rule Loading and Behavior Constraint Diagram**

### Constraint Enforcement Patterns

Rules constrain behavior through three mechanisms:

#### 1. Preventive Guidance (Proactive)

Rules steer initial decisions to avoid violations:

```markdown
# From coding-style.md
- Prefer functional patterns over classes
- Use pure functions for business logic
- Avoid mutable state

# Agent behavior:
# ✓ Generates functional code by default
# ✗ Does not reach for classes unless explicitly requested
```

#### 2. Violation Detection (Reactive)

Rules trigger self-correction when violations occur:

```markdown
# From security.md
- No console.log in production code

# Agent behavior:
# 1. Writes code with console.log during debugging
# 2. Detects violation against rule
# 3. Removes console.log before finalizing
```

#### 3. Process Enforcement (Workflow)

Rules establish mandatory workflows:

```markdown
# From testing.md
- Write tests before implementation (TDD)
- Run test suite before committing

# Agent behavior:
# 1. Writes test file (*.test.ts)
# 2. Runs test (should fail - RED)
# 3. Implements feature
# 4. Runs test (should pass - GREEN)
# 5. Refactors if needed
```

### Real-World Constraint Example

Consider a rule set for a Next.js SaaS project:

```markdown
# .claude/rules/patterns.md
- API routes in app/api/
- Database queries via server actions only
- Client components must have "use client" directive
- Server components by default (no directive)

# .claude/rules/security.md
- Never expose database credentials to client
- All API endpoints must validate session
- Rate limiting on public endpoints
```

**Without these rules:**
```typescript
// Potential violation: database query in client component
'use client'
import { db } from '@/lib/database'

export function UserList() {
  const users = db.query.users.findMany() // Exposes DB to client!
  // ...
}
```

**With rules enforced:**
```typescript
// Compliant: server action pattern
// app/actions/users.ts (server-side)
'use server'
export async function getUsers() {
  // Validated session check
  const session = await auth()
  if (!session) throw new Error('Unauthorized')
  
  return db.query.users.findMany()
}

// app/components/user-list.tsx (client)
'use client'
import { getUsers } from '@/actions/users'

export function UserList() {
  const users = use(getUsers()) // Server action, not direct DB access
  // ...
}
```

The agent self-corrects toward the server action pattern because the rules define it as the expected approach.

Sources: [README.md:636-649](), [the-shortform-guide.md:99-123]()

---

## Integration with Other Components

Rules interact with and complement other ECC components:

```mermaid
graph LR
    Rules["Rules<br/>(Always-Follow)"]
    
    Skills["Skills<br/>(Invoked)"]
    Agents["Agents<br/>(Delegated)"]
    Hooks["Hooks<br/>(Automated)"]
    Commands["Commands<br/>(User-Triggered)"]
    
    Rules -->|"Constrain"| Skills
    Rules -->|"Constrain"| Agents
    Rules -->|"Referenced by"| Hooks
    Rules -->|"Applied during"| Commands
    
    Skills -.->|"Example: tdd-workflow<br/>enforces testing.md"| Rules
    Agents -.->|"Example: code-reviewer<br/>checks security.md"| Rules
    Hooks -.->|"Example: post-edit-format<br/>enforces coding-style.md"| Rules
    Commands -.->|"Example: /verify<br/>validates against all rules"| Rules
```

**Rules Integration with ECC Components Diagram**

### Rules vs Skills

| Aspect | Rules | Skills |
|--------|-------|--------|
| **Activation** | Automatic (every session) | Manual invocation (`/tdd`, `/plan`) |
| **Scope** | Universal constraints | Workflow-specific guidance |
| **Content** | "Always do X" / "Never do Y" | "When doing Z, follow steps A→B→C" |
| **Example** | "Minimum 80% test coverage" | "TDD workflow: RED→GREEN→IMPROVE" |

**Relationship:** Skills often **implement** what rules **require**. For example:
- `rules/common/testing.md` requires 80% coverage
- `skills/tdd-workflow/` provides the process to achieve it

### Rules and Agents

Subagents inherit rules from the main agent's context. When a subagent is spawned:

```bash
# Main agent starts with all rules loaded
claude

# User delegates to code-reviewer subagent
"@code-reviewer Review the auth module"

# code-reviewer agent receives:
# - Its own agent definition (agents/code-reviewer.md)
# - All rules from ~/.claude/rules/ and .claude/rules/
# - The specific task context
```

This ensures subagents enforce the same standards. From [rules/common/agents.md]():

```markdown
# When to Delegate to Subagents

Delegate when:
- Task is well-scoped (single file, single concern)
- Requires specialized expertise (security review, E2E testing)
- Benefits from fresh context (avoid main agent's accumulated assumptions)

Do NOT delegate when:
- Task requires awareness of recent conversation context
- Cross-cutting changes across many files
- Ambiguous requirements needing clarification
```

### Rules and Hooks

Hooks **enforce** rules through automation. Common patterns:

| Rule Requirement | Hook Implementation |
|------------------|-------------------|
| "No console.log in commits" | PostToolUse hook greps for `console.log`, warns on stderr |
| "Format code before committing" | PostToolUse hook runs Prettier after Edit/Write |
| "80% test coverage" | Stop hook runs coverage check, blocks if below threshold |
| "No .md files except README/CLAUDE" | PreToolUse hook on Write blocks non-standard docs |

Hooks operationalize rule compliance. Without hooks, rules rely on the agent's self-monitoring. With hooks, violations trigger immediate feedback.

### Rules and Commands

Commands are constrained by rules but can also **verify** rule compliance:

```bash
# /verify command checks against all rules
/verify

# Internally runs:
# 1. Build (ensures code compiles)
# 2. Type check (TypeScript rules)
# 3. Lint (coding-style rules)
# 4. Test suite (testing rules)
# 5. Git status check (git-workflow rules)
```

The `/code-review` command explicitly references `rules/common/security.md` and `rules/common/patterns.md` when evaluating code.

Sources: [README.md:591-621](), [the-shortform-guide.md:14-35]()

---

## Platform Limitations and Manual Installation

### The Plugin Distribution Problem

**Critical Platform Constraint:** Claude Code's plugin system cannot distribute `rules` automatically. This is an [upstream limitation](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/plugins-reference) in the platform architecture.

```mermaid
graph TB
    Plugin[".claude-plugin/plugin.json"]
    
    Agents["agents/<br/>(Distributed ✓)"]
    Skills["skills/<br/>(Distributed ✓)"]
    Commands["commands/<br/>(Distributed ✓)"]
    Hooks["hooks/<br/>(Distributed ✓)"]
    Rules["rules/<br/>(NOT Distributed ✗)"]
    
    Plugin -->|"Automatic"| Agents
    Plugin -->|"Automatic"| Skills
    Plugin -->|"Automatic"| Commands
    Plugin -->|"Automatic"| Hooks
    Plugin -.->|"Platform limitation"| Rules
    
    Rules -->|"Requires"| ManualInstall["Manual Installation<br/>via cp or install.sh"]
    
    style Rules fill:#ffcccc
    style ManualInstall fill:#fff4cc
```

**Plugin Distribution Limitation Diagram**

### Manual Installation Methods

#### Method 1: Interactive Installer (Recommended)

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/affaan-m/everything-claude-code.git
cd everything-claude-code

# Run installer with language selection
./install.sh typescript        # Install common + TypeScript rules
./install.sh python golang     # Install common + Python + Go rules
./install.sh --target cursor typescript  # Cursor-specific installation
```

The `install.sh` script at [install.sh]() handles:
- Common rules installation (always included)
- Language-specific rule selection
- User-level vs project-level detection
- Merge vs overwrite conflict resolution

#### Method 2: Manual Copy

```bash
# User-level installation (applies to all projects)
mkdir -p ~/.claude/rules
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/common/* ~/.claude/rules/
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/typescript/* ~/.claude/rules/

# Project-level installation (current project only)
mkdir -p .claude/rules
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/common/* .claude/rules/
cp -r everything-claude-code/rules/python/* .claude/rules/
```

#### Method 3: Selective Installation

```bash
# Install only specific rule files
cp everything-claude-code/rules/common/security.md ~/.claude/rules/
cp everything-claude-code/rules/common/testing.md ~/.claude/rules/
cp everything-claude-code/rules/typescript/style.md ~/.claude/rules/
```

### Why This Limitation Exists

The Claude Code plugin system's security model prevents plugins from writing to user-level directories (`~/.claude/`) to avoid malicious plugin behavior. Rules must be explicitly placed by the user to ensure informed consent.

**Workaround Pattern (Community Convention):**

Most ECC users install the plugin for automatic component updates, then manually install rules once:

```bash
# Step 1: Plugin installation (auto-updates agents, skills, commands)
/plugin marketplace add affaan-m/everything-claude-code
/plugin install everything-claude-code@everything-claude-code

# Step 2: Manual rules installation (one-time setup)
git clone https://github.com/affaan-m/everything-claude-code.git
cd everything-claude-code
./install.sh typescript python
```

This hybrid approach balances convenience (plugin auto-updates) with security (manual rule installation).

Sources: [README.md:136-153](), [README.md:492-544]()

---

## Config Protection

While rules are read-only from the agent's perspective, some patterns attempt to circumvent rules by modifying configuration files (e.g., disabling linter rules instead of fixing code).

### Defense Mechanisms

The Plankton integration ([skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md]()) demonstrates config protection:

#### PreToolUse Hook — Blocks Config Edits

```json
{
  "PreToolUse": [{
    "matcher": "tool == \"Edit\" && (tool_input.file_path matches \"\\.ruff\\.toml$\" || tool_input.file_path matches \"biome\\.json$\")",
    "hooks": [{
      "type": "command",
      "command": "echo '[Hook] Cannot modify linter configs' >&2; exit 2"
    }]
  }]
}
```

Exit code 2 blocks the tool execution. The agent cannot proceed.

#### Stop Hook — Post-Session Detection

```bash
# stop_config_guardian.sh
git diff --name-only | grep -E '\\.ruff\\.toml|biome\\.json|shellcheckrc'
if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "[Hook] Config files modified - review required" >&2
  exit 2
fi
```

Detects config changes at session end, requiring manual review before commit.

### Protected File Patterns

Common patterns for config protection:

| File Pattern | Purpose |
|-------------|---------|
| `.ruff.toml` | Python linter config |
| `biome.json` | TypeScript formatter/linter |
| `.shellcheckrc` | Shell script linter |
| `.yamllint` | YAML linter |
| `.hadolint.yaml` | Dockerfile linter |
| `tsconfig.json` | TypeScript compiler options |
| `.eslintrc.json` | ESLint rules |

These files encode rule enforcement. Allowing agents to modify them would undermine the rule system.

Sources: [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:56-63]()

---

## Advanced Patterns

### Dynamic Rule Loading

For specialized sessions, inject rules dynamically via CLI flags:

```bash
# Research mode (relaxed coverage requirements)
claude --system-prompt "$(cat ~/.claude/contexts/research.md)"

# Production deployment mode (strict security)
claude --system-prompt "$(cat ~/.claude/contexts/production.md)"
```

This technique allows context-specific rule sets without modifying the default rule files. See [Memory & Context Preservation](#11.3) for full details.

### Rule Composition

Layer rules from multiple sources:

```bash
# User-level: personal baseline
~/.claude/rules/
  security.md
  testing.md

# Project-level: team conventions
.claude/rules/
  security.md       # Overrides user security.md
  api-patterns.md   # Project-specific addition

# Result: project security.md + project api-patterns.md + user testing.md
```

Project rules **replace** (not merge) user rules for the same file name. To extend instead of replace, use unique filenames:

```bash
~/.claude/rules/security-baseline.md    # User-level
.claude/rules/security-compliance.md    # Project-level (additive)
```

### Rule Verification

Use the `/verify` command to check if code complies with all rules:

```bash
/verify

# Internally checks:
# - Build passes (performance.md model selection)
# - Tests pass (testing.md coverage requirements)
# - Lint passes (coding-style.md conventions)
# - Git status clean (git-workflow.md commit requirements)
```

This command is the canonical "does this code satisfy the rules?" gate.

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:54-76](), [README.md:298-300]()

---

## Summary

Rules form the declarative constraint layer in the ECC architecture:

| Characteristic | Value |
|---------------|-------|
| **Purpose** | Always-follow guidelines that constrain behavior |
| **Structure** | Hierarchical (common + language-specific) |
| **Scope** | Universal (applies to all operations) |
| **Precedence** | Project-level > User-level > User messages |
| **Distribution** | Manual installation (platform limitation) |
| **Enforcement** | Soft constraints + hook automation |
| **Integration** | Constrains agents, skills, commands; verified by `/verify` |

For detailed rule contents and examples, see [Rules Reference](#5.4). For installation procedures, see [Rules Installation](#4.4).

