## Skill vs Command vs Agent

Understanding the distinction between these three component types:

| Component | Purpose | Invocation | Storage |
|-----------|---------|-----------|---------|
| **Skill** | Workflow definition, domain knowledge | Loaded by agents or commands | `skills/{name}/SKILL.md` |
| **Command** | Quick execution prompt | Direct user invocation with `/command` | `commands/{name}.md` |
| **Agent** | Scoped subagent with limited tools | Delegated by orchestrator | `agents/{name}.md` |

```mermaid
graph LR
    USER["User Types<br/>/tdd"]
    
    CMD["commands/tdd.md<br/>Command File"]
    
    AGENT["agents/tdd-guide.md<br/>Agent Definition"]
    
    SKILL["skills/tdd-workflow/SKILL.md<br/>Workflow Steps"]
    
    USER --> CMD
    CMD --> AGENT
    AGENT --> SKILL
    
    CMD -.->|"Contains"| PROMPT["User-facing prompt<br/>+ agent reference"]
    AGENT -.->|"Contains"| CONFIG["Model, tools, permissions<br/>+ skill references"]
    SKILL -.->|"Contains"| WORKFLOW["RED→GREEN→IMPROVE<br/>patterns and examples"]
```

**Example invocation chain:**
1. User runs `/tdd` [commands/tdd.md]()
2. Command invokes `tdd-guide` agent [agents/tdd-guide.md]()
3. Agent loads `tdd-workflow` skill [skills/tdd-workflow/SKILL.md]()
4. Agent follows the RED→GREEN→IMPROVE pattern defined in the skill

**Sources:** [the-shortform-guide.md:13-34](), [README.md:595-633]()

