## Project-Specific Rules

Rules can be scoped to individual projects by placing them in `.claude/rules/` relative to the project root.

### User-Level vs Project-Level

| Location | Scope | Use Case |
|----------|-------|----------|
| `~/.claude/rules/` | All projects | Universal coding standards, personal preferences |
| `.claude/rules/` | Current project only | Project-specific conventions, team standards, framework requirements |

### Priority and Merging

When both user-level and project-level rules exist:
1. Project-level rules take precedence for overlapping topics
2. Both sets are loaded and merged
3. More specific rules override general ones

### Example: Project-Specific Rules Structure

```bash
my-project/
├── .claude/
│   └── rules/
│       ├── coding-style.md       # Extends ~/.claude/rules/coding-style.md
│       ├── api-conventions.md    # Project-specific
│       └── database-schema.md    # Project-specific
└── src/
```

**Best practice:** Keep common/language rules at user-level, project conventions at project-level.

Sources: [README.md:535-544](), [skills/configure-ecc/SKILL.md:44-61]()

