## Quality Enforcement Pipeline

### Three-Tier Strategy

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Tier 1: Silent Auto-Fix"
        Format["post-edit-format.js<br/>Biome/Prettier<br/>Exit 0 always"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Tier 2: Immediate Warnings"
        Type["post-edit-typecheck.js<br/>tsc --noEmit<br/>Exit 0, stderr warnings"]
        Console["post-edit-console-warn.js<br/>Regex scan<br/>Exit 0, stderr warnings"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Tier 3: Session-Level Checks"
        StopHook["check-console-log.js<br/>Git modified files<br/>Stop hook"]
    end
    
    Edit["Edit Tool<br/>Completes"] --> Format
    Format --> Type
    Type --> Console
    Console --> Observe["observe.sh (async)"]
    
    Response["Response<br/>Complete"] --> StopHook
    
    style Format fill:#90EE90
    style Type fill:#ffffe0
    style Console fill:#ffffe0
    style StopHook fill:#ffd0d0
```

### Coverage Analysis

**What gets auto-fixed (Tier 1):**
- Code formatting (40-50% of style issues per Plankton documentation)
- Import organization
- Semicolons, quotes, spacing

**What gets warned (Tier 2):**
- TypeScript type errors
- Console.log statements (per-file)
- Build failures

**What gets session-level checks (Tier 3):**
- Console.log in all modified files
- Session length for continuous learning
- Git working tree state

See [Plankton Code Quality System](#11.8) for the complete three-phase architecture.

