## Best Practices

### 1. Error Handling

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Wrap in try/catch, never crash
try {
  const input = JSON.parse(data);
  // Logic here
} catch (err) {
  // Silently ignore - hook failure shouldn't break Claude Code
}

// ❌ BAD: Unhandled errors crash the hook
const input = JSON.parse(data); // May throw
```

### 2. Performance

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Quick validation
if (/pattern/.test(cmd)) {
  console.error('[Hook] Warning');
}

// ❌ BAD: Heavy synchronous work blocks tool execution
const result = expensiveOperation(); // Blocks for seconds
```

For heavy work, use `"async": true` in hooks.json.

### 3. Output Protocol

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Always passthrough stdin
console.log(data);

// ❌ BAD: Modified or missing output
console.log(JSON.stringify({ modified: true }));
```

### 4. User Messages

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Clear, actionable messages to stderr
console.error('[Hook] WARNING: Large file detected');
console.error('[Hook] Consider splitting into smaller files');

// ❌ BAD: Debug dumps to stderr
console.error(JSON.stringify(input));
```

### 5. Exit Codes

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Exit 2 for blocking, default 0 otherwise
if (shouldBlock) {
  console.error('[Hook] BLOCKED: ...');
  process.exit(2);
}
// Falls through to exit 0

// ❌ BAD: Exit 1 treated as error
process.exit(1); // Unclear intent
```

Sources: [scripts/hooks/doc-file-warning.js:1-29](), [tests/integration/hooks.test.js:175-330]()

