## Cross-Session Memory Bridge Pattern

The session management system enables autonomous loops by preserving context across process boundaries:

### Memory Bridge Architecture

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Session N"
        StartN["SessionStart hook"]
        WorkN["User work in Claude"]
        EndN["SessionEnd hook"]
        FileN["YYYY-MM-DD-abc12345-session.tmp"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Session N+1"
        StartN1["SessionStart hook"]
        WorkN1["Continue work"]
        EndN1["SessionEnd hook"]
        FileN1["YYYY-MM-DD-def67890-session.tmp"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Learned Knowledge"
        Instincts["Instincts<br/>(from continuous learning)"]
        Skills["Skills<br/>(from /evolve)"]
    end
    
    WorkN --> EndN
    EndN --> FileN
    FileN -.->|"loaded by<br/>getAllSessions({limit:1})"| StartN1
    StartN1 --> WorkN1
    
    Instincts -.->|"context injection"| StartN1
    Skills -.->|"workflow guidance"| StartN1
```

**Key Mechanism:** The SessionStart hook's `getAllSessions({limit: 1})` call retrieves the most recent session, creating a memory bridge even when the underlying Claude instance has no memory of previous interactions [scripts/hooks/session-start.js:41-60]().

**Autonomous Loop Compatibility:** This architecture enables workflows like Continuous Claude PR Loop and Ralphinho DAG (see [Autonomous Loop Patterns](#10.7)) where each `claude -p` invocation can access full project history.

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/session-start.js:1-167](), Diagram 5 from high-level architecture

