This document describes the strategic compaction system implemented in `suggest-compact.js`, which tracks tool call counts and recommends manual compaction at logical intervals. This is part of the session management system's optimization layer, designed to maintain context quality without arbitrary interruptions.

For information about the PreCompact hook that runs when compaction occurs, see [Session & Context Hooks](#6.4). For general session persistence mechanisms, see [Session Lifecycle & Persistence](#7.4).

---

## Purpose & Philosophy

The strategic compaction system advocates **manual compaction at logical checkpoints** rather than automatic compaction at arbitrary points. The core philosophy:

- **Auto-compact happens mid-task**: Automatic compaction triggers at fixed message counts, often disrupting active work
- **Context preservation**: Manual compaction allows you to compact after exploration phases, before execution phases
- **Milestone-based**: Compact after completing a feature, before starting the next one
- **Strategic timing**: Preserve the most relevant context by choosing when to reset

The system **suggests** compaction opportunities but never forces them, leaving the decision to the user.

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:1-14]()

---

## Architecture Overview

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Session Lifecycle"
        Start["Session Start<br/>(SessionStart hook)"]
        Tools["Tool Executions<br/>(Edit, Write, Read, Bash)"]
        Hook["PreToolUse Hook<br/>(suggest-compact.js)"]
        End["Session End"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Counter File System"
        CounterFile["Counter File<br/>getTempDir()/claude-tool-count-{sessionId}"]
        Read["fs.openSync('a+')<br/>Read current count"]
        Increment["count = parsed + 1"]
        Write["ftruncateSync(0)<br/>writeSync(count)"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Threshold Logic"
        Check1{"count == threshold<br/>(default: 50)"}
        Check2{"count > threshold<br/>&& (count-threshold) % 25 == 0"}
        Suggest1["log: 'consider /compact<br/>if transitioning phases'"]
        Suggest2["log: 'good checkpoint<br/>for /compact'"]
    end
    
    Start --> Tools
    Tools --> Hook
    Hook --> CounterFile
    CounterFile --> Read
    Read --> Increment
    Increment --> Write
    Write --> Check1
    Check1 -->|Yes| Suggest1
    Check1 -->|No| Check2
    Check2 -->|Yes| Suggest2
    Check2 -->|No| Tools
    Suggest1 --> Tools
    Suggest2 --> Tools
    Tools -->|Loop| Hook
    Tools -->|Done| End
```

**Counter File Path Resolution:**
- Session ID: `process.env.CLAUDE_SESSION_ID || 'default'`
- File path: `path.join(getTempDir(), \`claude-tool-count-\${sessionId}\`)`
- Temp directory varies by platform (from `os.tmpdir()`)

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:17-75](), [scripts/lib/utils.js:45-49]()

---

## Counter File Mechanics

### File Location & Naming

| Component | Implementation | Example |
|-----------|---------------|---------|
| Base directory | `getTempDir()` (from `os.tmpdir()`) | `/tmp` (Linux/macOS)<br/>`C:\Users\...\AppData\Local\Temp` (Windows) |
| Session ID source | `process.env.CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` | `test-session-abc12345` |
| Fallback ID | `'default'` | When `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` is unset |
| Filename pattern | `claude-tool-count-${sessionId}` | `claude-tool-count-abc12345` |

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:23-33]()

### Read-Increment-Write Cycle

The counter follows an atomic file descriptor pattern to reduce race conditions:

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> OpenFile: fs.openSync(counterFile, 'a+')
    OpenFile --> ReadCurrent: fs.readSync(fd, buf, 0, 64, 0)
    ReadCurrent --> ParseValue: parseInt(buf.toString().trim(), 10)
    ParseValue --> ValidateRange: Check if 0 < parsed <= 1,000,000
    ValidateRange --> IncrementValid: count = parsed + 1
    ValidateRange --> ResetInvalid: count = 1 (if invalid/corrupted)
    IncrementValid --> Truncate: fs.ftruncateSync(fd, 0)
    ResetInvalid --> Truncate
    Truncate --> WriteNew: fs.writeSync(fd, String(count), 0)
    WriteNew --> CloseFile: fs.closeSync(fd)
    CloseFile --> [*]
```

**Race Condition Mitigation:**
- Uses `fs.openSync()` with `'a+'` mode (open for appending, create if not exists)
- Single file descriptor for read → truncate → write sequence
- Reduces (but does not eliminate) race window between concurrent hook invocations
- Fallback to `writeFile()` if fd operations fail

**Value Validation:**
- Valid range: `0 < count <= 1,000,000`
- Protects against corrupted files with extremely large integers
- Resets to `1` if parsed value is `NaN`, non-finite, or out of range

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:35-62]()

---

## Threshold & Interval System

### Initial Threshold

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    EnvVar["COMPACT_THRESHOLD<br/>environment variable"]
    Default["Default: 50"]
    Parse["parseInt(value, 10)"]
    Validate{"isFinite && > 0<br/>&& <= 10,000"}
    
    EnvVar --> Parse
    Parse --> Validate
    Validate -->|Pass| UseValue["threshold = parsed"]
    Validate -->|Fail| UseDefault["threshold = 50"]
    Default -->|Not set| UseDefault
```

**Threshold Configuration:**

| Configuration | Value Range | Default | Validation |
|---------------|-------------|---------|------------|
| `COMPACT_THRESHOLD` | 1 - 10,000 | 50 | Must be finite, positive, <= 10,000 |
| Invalid values | Any other | Falls back to 50 | Includes `NaN`, negative, zero, > 10,000 |

**First Suggestion:**
- Triggers when `count === threshold` (exact match)
- Message: `"${threshold} tool calls reached - consider /compact if transitioning phases"`
- One-time suggestion at the threshold

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:30-33](), [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:64-67]()

### Interval Suggestions

After the initial threshold, the system suggests compaction at **regular 25-call intervals**:

**Calculation:**
```javascript
if (count > threshold && (count - threshold) % 25 === 0)
```

**Example Progression (threshold = 50):**

| Tool Calls | Suggestion? | Message |
|------------|-------------|---------|
| 50 | ✅ Yes | "50 tool calls reached - consider /compact if transitioning phases" |
| 51-74 | ❌ No | (Silent) |
| 75 | ✅ Yes | "75 tool calls - good checkpoint for /compact if context is stale" |
| 76-99 | ❌ No | (Silent) |
| 100 | ✅ Yes | "100 tool calls - good checkpoint for /compact if context is stale" |
| 125 | ✅ Yes | "125 tool calls - good checkpoint for /compact if context is stale" |

**Interval Logic:**
- Only triggers when `count > threshold` (after initial suggestion)
- Uses modulo arithmetic: `(count - threshold) % 25 === 0`
- Ensures suggestions at 75, 100, 125, 150, 175, 200, etc.

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:69-72]()

---

## Hook Integration

### PreToolUse Hook Registration

The `suggest-compact.js` script is registered as a **PreToolUse hook** in `hooks.json`:

**Hook Configuration Pattern:**
```json
{
  "PreToolUse": [
    {
      "matcher": "*",
      "hooks": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "node scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Execution Flow:**

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant Claude
    participant HookEngine as Hook Engine
    participant SuggestCompact as suggest-compact.js
    participant Counter as Counter File
    
    User->>Claude: Invoke tool (Edit/Write/Read/Bash)
    Claude->>HookEngine: PreToolUse event
    HookEngine->>SuggestCompact: Execute hook
    SuggestCompact->>Counter: Read current count
    Counter-->>SuggestCompact: Return count (e.g., 49)
    SuggestCompact->>Counter: Write count + 1 (50)
    SuggestCompact->>SuggestCompact: Check if count == 50
    SuggestCompact->>Claude: log() to stderr (if threshold)
    SuggestCompact-->>HookEngine: Exit 0 (always allow)
    HookEngine->>Claude: Continue tool execution
    Claude->>User: Tool result + suggestion (if any)
```

**Exit Code Behavior:**
- Always exits with `0` (never blocks tool execution)
- Unlike validation hooks (e.g., `doc-file-warning.js`), this is purely advisory
- Logs to stderr for user visibility but doesn't modify stdin/stdout data flow

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:75](), [tests/hooks/hooks.test.js:341-348]()

---

## Configuration

### Environment Variables

| Variable | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` | string | `'default'` | Session identifier for counter file naming |
| `COMPACT_THRESHOLD` | integer | `50` | Number of tool calls before first suggestion |

**Setting COMPACT_THRESHOLD:**

```bash
# Bash/Zsh (Linux/macOS)
export COMPACT_THRESHOLD=100
claude -p

# PowerShell (Windows)
$env:COMPACT_THRESHOLD=100
claude -p

# One-time override
COMPACT_THRESHOLD=30 claude -p
```

**Session ID Resolution:**
- Sourced from `process.env.CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`
- If unset or empty, defaults to `'default'`
- Affects counter file name: `claude-tool-count-${sessionId}`
- Multiple concurrent sessions maintain separate counters

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:28-33]()

### Counter File Cleanup

Counter files persist in the temp directory across sessions. They are **not automatically cleaned up**:

**Manual Cleanup:**
```bash
# List all counter files
ls /tmp/claude-tool-count-*  # Unix
dir %TEMP%\claude-tool-count-*  # Windows

# Remove all counter files
rm /tmp/claude-tool-count-*  # Unix
del %TEMP%\claude-tool-count-*  # Windows

# Remove specific session
rm /tmp/claude-tool-count-abc12345  # Unix
del %TEMP%\claude-tool-count-abc12345  # Windows
```

**File Lifecycle:**
- Created on first tool use in a session
- Persists until manually deleted or system temp cleanup
- Reused across multiple invocations of the same session ID
- Each session ID gets its own counter file

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:23-33]()

---

## Edge Cases & Error Handling

### Corrupted Counter Files

**Scenario:** Counter file contains non-numeric or invalid data

**Handling:**

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Read["fs.readSync(fd, buf)"] --> Parse["parseInt(buf.toString().trim(), 10)"]
    Parse --> Check{"Number.isFinite(parsed)<br/>&& parsed > 0<br/>&& parsed <= 1,000,000"}
    Check -->|True| Use["count = parsed + 1"]
    Check -->|False| Reset["count = 1"]
    Use --> Write["fs.writeSync(fd, String(count))"]
    Reset --> Write
```

**Validation Rules:**
1. Must be finite number (rejects `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity`)
2. Must be positive (rejects `<= 0`)
3. Must be reasonable (rejects `> 1,000,000`)
4. Resets to `1` if any rule fails

**Example Corrupted Values:**

| File Content | Parsed Value | Behavior |
|--------------|--------------|----------|
| `"not-a-number"` | `NaN` | Reset to `1` |
| `""` (empty) | `NaN` | Reset to `1` |
| `"999999999999"` | `1e+12` | Reset to `1` (exceeds max) |
| `"-5"` | `-5` | Reset to `1` (negative) |
| `"0"` | `0` | Reset to `1` (not positive) |
| `"42"` | `42` | Valid, increment to `43` |

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:43-51](), [tests/hooks/hooks.test.js:428-446]()

### File Descriptor Failures

**Scenario:** `fs.openSync()`, `fs.readSync()`, or `fs.writeSync()` throws an error

**Fallback Mechanism:**

```javascript
try {
  const fd = fs.openSync(counterFile, 'a+');
  try {
    // ... fd-based read/write operations ...
  } finally {
    fs.closeSync(fd);
  }
} catch {
  // Fallback: just use writeFile if fd operations fail
  writeFile(counterFile, String(count));
}
```

**Fallback Behavior:**
- Catches all errors in fd operations
- Falls back to simple `writeFile(counterFile, '1')`
- Sets count to `1` (assumes fresh start)
- Hook continues execution (does not crash)

**Common Failure Causes:**
- Permission denied on temp directory
- Temp directory does not exist
- Disk full
- File system errors

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:40-62]()

### Default Session ID

**Scenario:** `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` environment variable is not set

**Behavior:**

```javascript
const sessionId = process.env.CLAUDE_SESSION_ID || 'default';
const counterFile = path.join(getTempDir(), `claude-tool-count-${sessionId}`);
```

**Implications:**
- All sessions without explicit `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` share the same counter
- Counter file: `claude-tool-count-default`
- Tool call count accumulates across multiple `claude -p` invocations
- Can lead to unexpected suggestion timing if using default session ID

**Recommendation:**
- Claude Code automatically sets `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` per session
- Users invoking `claude` CLI directly should set unique session IDs for accurate tracking

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:28](), [tests/hooks/hooks.test.js:448-458]()

---

## Integration with Session Lifecycle

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Session N"
        Start1["SessionStart Hook"]
        Counter1["Counter: 0 → 50"]
        Suggest1["Suggest /compact"]
        Work1["Continue work"]
        End1["SessionEnd Hook"]
    end
    
    subgraph "User Action"
        Compact["/compact command"]
        ContextReset["Context window reset"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Session N+1"
        Start2["SessionStart Hook"]
        Counter2["Counter: 50 → 75"]
        Suggest2["Suggest /compact"]
        Work2["Continue work"]
        End2["SessionEnd Hook"]
    end
    
    Start1 --> Counter1
    Counter1 --> Suggest1
    Suggest1 --> Work1
    Work1 --> End1
    
    End1 -.->|User decides| Compact
    Compact --> ContextReset
    ContextReset --> Start2
    
    End1 -.->|User ignores| Start2
    
    Start2 --> Counter2
    Counter2 --> Suggest2
    Suggest2 --> Work2
    Work2 --> End2
```

**Key Points:**
- Counter persists across multiple session invocations (same `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`)
- `/compact` command does not reset the counter
- Counter only resets when:
  - Counter file is manually deleted
  - Temp directory is cleaned by OS
  - New session ID is used

**Pre-Compact Hook Integration:**
- When user runs `/compact`, the `PreCompact` hook runs
- `pre-compact.js` annotates the session file with compaction marker
- Compaction log is created in `~/.claude/sessions/compaction-log.txt`
- Counter file is **not affected** by compaction

**Sources:** [scripts/hooks/suggest-compact.js:23-33](), [scripts/hooks/pre-compact.js]() (referenced in [Session & Context Hooks](#6.4))

