## Phase 2: Analysis

### Observer Daemon Architecture

The `start-observer.sh` daemon runs as a detached background process with a PID file and log file. It monitors the `observations.jsonl` file and triggers analysis when thresholds are met.

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Observer Process"
        MainLoop["Main Loop<br/>(sleep + trap USR1)"]
        CheckThreshold{"Count ≥ min_observations<br/>(default 20)?"}
        ClaudeInvoke["claude --model haiku --max-turns 3<br/>Analyze observations"]
        CreateInstinct["Create/Update<br/>instinct files"]
        Archive["Archive processed<br/>observations.jsonl"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Configuration"
        ConfigJSON["config.json<br/>observer.enabled<br/>run_interval_minutes<br/>min_observations_to_analyze"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Signals"
        USR1["kill -USR1<br/>from observe.sh"]
        InterruptSleep["Kill pending sleep<br/>Analyze immediately"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Storage"
        PIDFile[".observer.pid<br/>(project-scoped)"]
        LogFile["observer.log<br/>(stderr/stdout)"]
        ObsFile["observations.jsonl"]
        InstinctsDir["instincts/personal/"]
    end
    
    ConfigJSON --> MainLoop
    MainLoop --> CheckThreshold
    CheckThreshold -->|No| MainLoop
    CheckThreshold -->|Yes| ClaudeInvoke
    ClaudeInvoke --> CreateInstinct
    CreateInstinct --> Archive
    Archive --> MainLoop
    
    USR1 --> InterruptSleep
    InterruptSleep --> CheckThreshold
    
    MainLoop -.reads.-> PIDFile
    MainLoop -.writes.-> LogFile
    CheckThreshold -.reads.-> ObsFile
    CreateInstinct -.writes.-> InstinctsDir
```

**Observer process lifecycle:**

```bash
# Start observer for current project
start-observer.sh start
# → Forks background process with nohup
# → Writes PID to ${PROJECT_DIR}/.observer.pid
# → Logs to ${PROJECT_DIR}/observer.log

# Check status
start-observer.sh status
# → Checks if PID is alive (kill -0)

# Stop observer
start-observer.sh stop
# → Sends SIGTERM to PID
```

**Sources:** [skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/start-observer.sh:1-285]()

### Pattern Detection Logic

The observer invokes Claude Haiku with a structured prompt that specifies four pattern types:

| Pattern Type | Detection Criteria | Confidence Initial | Example |
|--------------|-------------------|-------------------|---------|
| **User Corrections** | Follow-up message contradicts prior action | 0.5 (3-5 obs) | "No, use hooks not classes" |
| **Error Resolutions** | Error in tool output → fix in next tools | 0.5 (3-5 obs) | "When TypeError → add type guard" |
| **Repeated Workflows** | Same tool sequence 3+ times | 0.7 (6-10 obs) | "Grep → Read → Edit" |
| **Tool Preferences** | Consistent tool choice for task | 0.85 (11+ obs) | "Always use Read before Edit" |

The prompt specifies exact YAML frontmatter requirements and confidence scoring rules:

```yaml
---
id: kebab-case-name
trigger: "when <specific condition>"
confidence: <0.3-0.85 based on frequency>
domain: <code-style|testing|git|debugging|workflow|file-patterns>
source: session-observation
scope: <project|global>
project_id: <hash if project scope>
project_name: <name if project scope>
---
```

**Scope decision heuristics** (executed by Claude Haiku):

```python
# Pseudo-code from observer prompt
if pattern_about_language_framework or pattern_about_file_structure:
    scope = "project"
elif pattern_about_security or pattern_about_general_practices:
    scope = "global"
else:
    scope = "project"  # Default to project, promote later if needed
```

**Sources:** [skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/start-observer.sh:168-232](), [skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/observer.md:30-149]()

