## Workflow Orchestration Patterns

### Interactive vs Autonomous Execution

ECC workflows operate in two modes:

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Interactive Mode"
        User["User Input<br/>(chat messages)"]
        Claude1["Claude Response"]
        Review1["User Review"]
        Iterate1["Next Instruction"]
        
        User --> Claude1
        Claude1 --> Review1
        Review1 --> Iterate1
        Iterate1 --> User
    end
    
    subgraph "Autonomous Mode"
        Script["Shell Script<br/>(daily-dev.sh)"]
        ClaudeP1["claude -p 'step 1'"]
        ClaudeP2["claude -p 'step 2'"]
        ClaudeP3["claude -p 'step 3'"]
        Exit["Exit on Failure<br/>(set -e)"]
        
        Script --> ClaudeP1
        ClaudeP1 --> ClaudeP2
        ClaudeP2 --> ClaudeP3
        ClaudeP1 -.->|"exit != 0"| Exit
        ClaudeP2 -.->|"exit != 0"| Exit
        ClaudeP3 -.->|"exit != 0"| Exit
    end
    
    style User fill:#e1f5ff
    style Script fill:#ffe1f5
```

**Interactive workflows** (Feature Dev, TDD, Review) require user guidance between steps. The user invokes commands and reviews results before proceeding.

**Autonomous workflows** (Autonomous Loops [#10.7](#10.7)) execute without user intervention via `claude -p` pipelines or continuous loops. Each step is a separate Claude invocation with fresh context.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:35-102](), [the-longform-guide.md:175-203]()

### Hook-Driven Automation

Workflows integrate with the hook system for automated quality enforcement:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant Workflow as "Workflow Step"
    participant PreHook as "PreToolUse Hooks"
    participant Tool as "Tool Execution"
    participant PostHook as "PostToolUse Hooks"
    participant Learning as "Continuous Learning"
    
    User->>Workflow: Execute workflow step<br/>(e.g., /tdd)
    Workflow->>Tool: Edit file
    
    Tool->>PreHook: doc-file-warning.js<br/>dev-server-tmux.sh
    PreHook-->>Tool: Allow (exit 0) or Block (exit 2)
    
    Tool->>Tool: Execute file edit
    
    Tool->>PostHook: post-edit-format.js<br/>post-edit-typecheck.js<br/>post-edit-console-warn.js
    PostHook->>PostHook: Auto-format with biome/prettier<br/>TypeScript type check<br/>Warn about console.log
    PostHook->>Learning: observe.sh captures tool use
    PostHook-->>Workflow: Warnings to stderr (exit 0)
    
    Workflow->>User: Step complete (with warnings)
    
    Note over Learning: Async pattern detection<br/>for continuous learning
```

Workflows benefit from transparent quality enforcement:
- **PreToolUse hooks**: Validation before actions (prevent dev servers outside tmux, warn before git push)
- **PostToolUse hooks**: Auto-formatting, type checking, linting (40-50% of issues fixed silently)
- **SessionEnd hooks**: Learning extraction, session persistence

Sources: [hooks/hooks.json]() (referenced), [README.md:339-355](), [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:20-56]()

