## Best Practices

### Workflow Design Principles

1. **Start with research** — Use Search-First [#10.2](#10.2) before implementing from scratch
2. **Plan before coding** — The planner agent front-loads thinking to save implementation cycles
3. **Test-first mindset** — TDD [#10.3](#10.3) catches issues early when they're cheap to fix
4. **Separate concerns** — Use different agents for implementation vs review (eliminates author bias)
5. **Incremental verification** — Use checkpoints throughout, not just at the end
6. **Context bridges** — For multi-session workflows, use session files or SHARED_TASK_NOTES.md
7. **Model routing** — Default to Sonnet, escalate to Opus for complex/security-critical work

Sources: [rules/common/development-workflow.md:1-38](), [the-longform-guide.md:206-286](), [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:521-541]()

### Anti-Patterns to Avoid

| Anti-Pattern | Problem | Solution |
|--------------|---------|----------|
| Jumping to code | Wastes time on solved problems | Use Search-First [#10.2](#10.2) |
| No test coverage | Bugs discovered in production | Use TDD [#10.3](#10.3) workflow |
| Single-pass implementation | Misses quality issues | Add review step [#10.4](#10.4) |
| Context loss between sessions | Re-explaining work each time | Use session persistence |
| All work in one context | Author bias in reviews | Use separate reviewer agents |
| No verification gates | Broken builds reach main | Use /verify and /checkpoint |

Sources: [skills/search-first/SKILL.md:156-161](), [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:582-596]()

