## Anti-Patterns

Common mistakes that violate the search-first principle.

| Anti-Pattern | Description | Impact | Remedy |
|--------------|-------------|--------|--------|
| **Jumping to code** | Writing a utility without checking if one exists | Waste time reinventing solved problems | Always run Quick Mode checklist first |
| **Ignoring MCP** | Not checking if an MCP server provides the capability | Miss pre-integrated tools | Check `~/.claude/settings.json` before searching npm/PyPI |
| **Over-customizing** | Wrapping a library so heavily it loses its benefits | Maintain custom code instead of using library updates | Use EXTEND sparingly, prefer ADOPT when possible |
| **Dependency bloat** | Installing a massive package for one small feature | Increase bundle size, slow installs | Evaluate bundle size, consider COMPOSE (multiple small packages) or BUILD (minimal custom) |
| **Skipping evaluation** | Installing the first result without comparing | Miss better alternatives | Always evaluate at least top 3 candidates |
| **Ignoring maintenance** | Choosing abandoned libraries with high star count | Security vulnerabilities, compatibility issues | Check last commit date, issue response time |

Sources: [skills/search-first/SKILL.md:156-162]()

