## Review Workflow Patterns

### Pattern 1: Feature Development Review Cycle

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Dev as Developer
    participant TDD as tdd-guide agent
    participant Code as Main Agent
    participant Hooks as PostToolUse Hooks
    participant Review as code-reviewer agent
    
    Dev->>TDD: /tdd "Add authentication"
    TDD->>Code: Write failing tests
    Code->>Hooks: Edit test files
    Hooks-->>Code: Format + typecheck (auto)
    
    TDD->>Code: Implement feature
    Code->>Hooks: Edit source files
    Hooks-->>Code: Format + typecheck (auto)
    
    Dev->>Review: /code-review
    Review->>Review: Read changed files<br/>Analyze patterns<br/>Check security
    Review->>Dev: review-comments.md<br/>(prioritized issues)
    
    Dev->>Code: Address review issues
    Code->>Hooks: Edit files
    Hooks-->>Code: Format + typecheck (auto)
    
    Dev->>Review: /code-review
    Review->>Dev: ✓ No blocking issues
```

**Diagram: TDD → Implement → Review Cycle**

This is the canonical workflow for new features:
1. TDD agent enforces test-first development
2. Write-time hooks catch formatting and type issues immediately
3. Manual review catches logical issues, security vulnerabilities, edge cases
4. Fix → re-review loop until clean

Sources: [README.md:673-686](), [the-longform-guide.md:271-287]()

### Pattern 2: Autonomous Review Pipeline

In autonomous loops (see [Autonomous Loop Patterns](#10.7)), review agents are inserted as pipeline stages with separate context windows to eliminate author bias:

```mermaid
graph LR
    Research["research stage<br/>(Sonnet)"]
    Plan["plan stage<br/>(Opus)"]
    Implement["implement stage<br/>(Codex)"]
    Test["test stage<br/>(Sonnet)"]
    PRDReview["PRD-review stage<br/>(Sonnet)"]
    CodeReview["code-review stage<br/>(Opus)"]
    Fix["review-fix stage<br/>(Codex)"]
    
    Research --> Plan
    Plan --> Implement
    Implement --> Test
    Test --> PRDReview
    Test --> CodeReview
    PRDReview --> Fix
    CodeReview --> Fix
    Fix --> Test
```

**Diagram: Ralphinho Pipeline with Separate Review Context**

Key design principle: **The reviewer never wrote the code it reviews**. Each stage runs in its own agent process with its own context window. This eliminates the most common source of missed issues — author bias during self-review.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:378-467]()

### Pattern 3: Multi-Tier Review Depth

Different complexity levels warrant different review depths:

| Tier | Review Pipeline |
|------|----------------|
| **trivial** | implement → test |
| **small** | implement → test → code-review |
| **medium** | research → plan → implement → test → PRD-review + code-review → review-fix |
| **large** | research → plan → implement → test → PRD-review + code-review → review-fix → **final-review** |

This prevents expensive Opus review on simple changes while ensuring architectural changes get thorough scrutiny.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:438-449]()

