## Write-Time Quality Enforcement

### PostToolUse Hook Pipeline

After every `Edit` or `Write` tool execution, a chain of PostToolUse hooks runs:

```mermaid
graph TB
    EditTool["Edit/Write Tool Completes"]
    
    FormatCheck{"File matches format patterns?"}
    TypeCheck{"File is .ts/.tsx?"}
    ConsoleCheck{"File is .ts/.tsx/.js/.jsx?"}
    
    Format["post-edit-format.js<br/>Run biome/prettier"]
    Type["post-edit-typecheck.js<br/>Run tsc --noEmit"]
    Console["post-edit-console-warn.js<br/>grep for console.log"]
    
    FormatStderr["Write formatted diff to stderr<br/>(non-blocking)"]
    TypeStderr["Write type errors to stderr<br/>(non-blocking)"]
    ConsoleStderr["Write console.log warnings to stderr<br/>(non-blocking)"]
    
    Observe["observe.sh<br/>Capture for learning"]
    
    EditTool --> FormatCheck
    EditTool --> TypeCheck
    EditTool --> ConsoleCheck
    EditTool --> Observe
    
    FormatCheck -->|Yes| Format
    FormatCheck -->|No| Observe
    Format --> FormatStderr
    FormatStderr --> Observe
    
    TypeCheck -->|Yes| Type
    TypeCheck -->|No| Observe
    Type --> TypeStderr
    TypeStderr --> Observe
    
    ConsoleCheck -->|Yes| Console
    ConsoleCheck -->|No| Observe
    Console --> ConsoleStderr
    ConsoleStderr --> Observe
```

**Diagram: PostToolUse Hook Execution Flow**

All hooks are **advisory** (exit code 0) — they warn via stderr but don't block execution. The main agent receives feedback and can choose to fix issues or proceed.

Sources: [the-shortform-guide.md:67-73](), [README.md:338-353]()

### Plankton Integration: Three-Phase Quality Enforcement

Plankton (credit: @alxfazio) provides more aggressive write-time enforcement via a three-phase PostToolUse hook:

```mermaid
graph TB
    FileEdit["File Edit Event"]
    
    Phase1["Phase 1: Auto-Format (Silent)<br/>ruff format, biome, shfmt, taplo<br/>Fixes 40-50% of issues<br/>No output to agent"]
    
    Phase2["Phase 2: Collect Violations (JSON)<br/>Run linters, structure output<br/>Still no output to agent"]
    
    ViolationsCheck{"Violations found?"}
    
    Phase3["Phase 3: Delegate + Verify<br/>Spawn claude -p subprocess"]
    
    ModelRoute{"Violation complexity?"}
    
    Haiku["Haiku subprocess<br/>(120s timeout)<br/>E/W/F codes: imports, style"]
    Sonnet["Sonnet subprocess<br/>(300s timeout)<br/>C901, PLR: complexity, refactor"]
    Opus["Opus subprocess<br/>(600s timeout)<br/>Type system, deep reasoning"]
    
    Verify["Re-run Phase 1+2<br/>Verify fixes"]
    
    Clean{"Clean?"}
    
    Exit0["Exit 0<br/>(transparent to agent)"]
    Exit2["Exit 2 + stderr<br/>(report to agent)"]
    
    FileEdit --> Phase1
    Phase1 --> Phase2
    Phase2 --> ViolationsCheck
    
    ViolationsCheck -->|No| Exit0
    ViolationsCheck -->|Yes| Phase3
    
    Phase3 --> ModelRoute
    ModelRoute -->|Simple| Haiku
    ModelRoute -->|Medium| Sonnet
    ModelRoute -->|Complex| Opus
    
    Haiku --> Verify
    Sonnet --> Verify
    Opus --> Verify
    
    Verify --> Clean
    Clean -->|Yes| Exit0
    Clean -->|No| Exit2
```

**Diagram: Plankton Three-Phase Quality Pipeline**

The main agent only sees issues the subprocess couldn't fix. Most quality problems (40-50%) are resolved transparently in Phase 1.

Sources: [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:18-55]()

