## Quality Metrics and Observability

### Hook Performance Tracking

The `observe.sh` hook (see [Observation System](#8.2)) captures quality enforcement metrics:

```json
{
  "tool": "Edit",
  "file": "src/auth/login.ts",
  "timestamp": "2026-02-15T10:30:00Z",
  "hooks_executed": [
    "post-edit-format.js",
    "post-edit-typecheck.js"
  ],
  "format_issues_fixed": 3,
  "type_errors": 0,
  "console_logs": 1,
  "time_ms": 45
}
```

This data feeds the continuous learning system to identify patterns like:
- "TypeScript files in src/api/ frequently have import ordering issues"
- "Database query files often trigger SQL injection warnings"

These patterns become instincts that guide future development.

Sources: [README.md:338-353]()

### Review Coverage Analysis

Track which files receive manual review vs automated review only:

```bash
# Files changed in last 10 commits
git diff --name-only HEAD~10 HEAD > changed-files.txt

# Files reviewed by code-reviewer agent (check git log for /code-review invocations)
git log --grep="code-review" --since="1 week ago" --format="%H" | \
  xargs -I {} git show --name-only {} | \
  grep -v "^commit\|^Author\|^Date\|^$" > reviewed-files.txt

# Files with only automated review (hooks only)
comm -23 <(sort changed-files.txt) <(sort reviewed-files.txt) > hook-only-files.txt
```

High-risk files (auth, payments, database migrations) should receive manual review, not just hook enforcement.

