## Metrics Calculation

### Pass@N Calculation

For capability evals, the `pass@N` metric measures success rate within N attempts:

| Eval | Attempt 1 | Attempt 2 | Attempt 3 | Contributes to |
|------|-----------|-----------|-----------|----------------|
| eval-1 | ✅ PASS | - | - | pass@1, pass@2, pass@3 |
| eval-2 | ❌ FAIL | ✅ PASS | - | pass@2, pass@3 |
| eval-3 | ❌ FAIL | ❌ FAIL | ✅ PASS | pass@3 |
| eval-4 | ❌ FAIL | ❌ FAIL | ❌ FAIL | none |

**Calculation:**
- `pass@1 = 1/4 = 25%`
- `pass@2 = 2/4 = 50%`
- `pass@3 = 3/4 = 75%`

**Result:** Does not meet threshold (pass@3 > 90%)

### Pass^N Calculation

For regression evals, the `pass^N` metric requires N consecutive successful runs:

| Run | eval-1 | eval-2 | eval-3 | Consecutive Count |
|-----|--------|--------|--------|-------------------|
| 1 | ✅ PASS | ✅ PASS | ✅ PASS | 1 |
| 2 | ✅ PASS | ✅ PASS | ❌ FAIL | reset to 0 for eval-3 |
| 3 | ✅ PASS | ✅ PASS | ✅ PASS | 1 for eval-3, 3 for eval-1 and eval-2 |

**Calculation:**
- `eval-1: pass^3 = 3 consecutive ✅`
- `eval-2: pass^3 = 3 consecutive ✅`
- `eval-3: pass^3 = 1 consecutive ❌ (needs 3)`

**Result:** Does not meet threshold (pass^3 = 100%)

**Sources:** [commands/eval.md:86-89]()

## Workflow Patterns

### Pattern 1: Feature Development

```mermaid
graph TB
    START["Start new feature"]
    DEFINE["/eval define feature-payments"]
    PLAN["/plan Implement payment flow"]
    
    subgraph "Implementation Loop"
        CODE["Write code"]
        CHECK["/eval check feature-payments"]
        FIX["Fix failing evals"]
    end
    
    VERIFY["/verify full"]
    REPORT["/eval report feature-payments"]
    DECISION{Ship decision?}
    
    START --> DEFINE
    DEFINE --> PLAN
    PLAN --> CODE
    CODE --> CHECK
    CHECK -->|"Not all passing"| FIX
    FIX --> CODE
    CHECK -->|"All passing"| VERIFY
    VERIFY --> REPORT
    REPORT --> DECISION
    DECISION -->|"SHIP"| MERGE["Merge PR"]
    DECISION -->|"NEEDS WORK"| FIX
```

### Pattern 2: Bug Fix with Regression Guard

```mermaid
graph TB
    BUG["Bug reported"]
    DEFINE["/eval define bugfix-auth-leak"]
    
    subgraph "Eval Definition"
        CAP["Capability: Bug is fixed"]
        REG["Regression: Related features work"]
    end
    
    FIX["Implement fix"]
    CHECK["/eval check bugfix-auth-leak"]
    ITERATE{All evals passing?}
    REPORT["/eval report bugfix-auth-leak"]
    
    BUG --> DEFINE
    DEFINE -.defines.-> CAP
    DEFINE -.defines.-> REG
    DEFINE --> FIX
    FIX --> CHECK
    CHECK --> ITERATE
    ITERATE -->|"No"| FIX
    ITERATE -->|"Yes"| REPORT
```

### Pattern 3: Refactoring Verification

```mermaid
graph TB
    START["Plan refactoring"]
    DEFINE["/eval define refactor-caching"]
    
    subgraph "Regression-Heavy"
        REG1["All existing tests pass"]
        REG2["Performance maintained"]
        REG3["API contracts unchanged"]
        CAP1["Code complexity reduced"]
    end
    
    BASELINE["/checkpoint create pre-refactor"]
    REFACTOR["Refactor code"]
    CHECK["/eval check refactor-caching"]
    COMPARE["/checkpoint verify pre-refactor"]
    REPORT["/eval report refactor-caching"]
    
    START --> DEFINE
    DEFINE -.defines.-> REG1
    DEFINE -.defines.-> REG2
    DEFINE -.defines.-> REG3
    DEFINE -.defines.-> CAP1
    DEFINE --> BASELINE
    BASELINE --> REFACTOR
    REFACTOR --> CHECK
    CHECK --> COMPARE
    COMPARE --> REPORT
```

**Sources:** [commands/eval.md:1-121](), [commands/checkpoint.md:1-75]()

## Best Practices

### Defining Effective Capability Evals

**DO:**
- Define specific, measurable criteria before implementation
- Include edge cases and error conditions
- Make evals independent and atomic
- Use concrete examples ("User can login with email") over abstract goals ("Auth works")

**DON'T:**
- Define evals after implementation (defeats the purpose)
- Make evals too broad ("Entire feature works")
- Create interdependent evals that can't run in isolation

### Defining Effective Regression Evals

**DO:**
- Cover all integration points touched by the change
- Include performance benchmarks where relevant
- Test API contracts and public interfaces
- Verify data integrity and consistency

**DON'T:**
- Skip regression evals for "simple" changes
- Assume existing tests are sufficient (make it explicit)
- Ignore indirect effects on other systems

### Running Evals Efficiently

**DO:**
- Check evals frequently during development (after each significant change)
- Use `/verify quick` for fast iteration, `/verify full` before reporting
- Keep eval log files for historical analysis
- Clean up old logs periodically with `/eval clean`

**DON'T:**
- Wait until "done" to run first eval check
- Ignore early failures hoping they'll resolve later
- Run full eval suite on every minor change

### Interpreting Results

**Ship Decision Matrix:**

| Capability pass@3 | Regression pass^3 | Decision |
|-------------------|-------------------|----------|
| < 90% | Any | **NEEDS WORK** - Core functionality unreliable |
| ≥ 90% | < 100% | **BLOCKED** - Regression risk unacceptable |
| ≥ 90% | 100% | **SHIP** - Ready for production |

**Pass@1 as Quality Indicator:**
- `pass@1 > 80%`: High quality, minimal retries needed
- `pass@1 50-80%`: Acceptable quality, some brittleness
- `pass@1 < 50%`: Poor quality, investigate root causes

**Sources:** [commands/eval.md:1-121]()

## Cleanup and Maintenance

The eval system accumulates logs over time. The `/eval clean` command removes old evaluation logs while preserving the most recent 10 runs per feature. This maintains historical context while preventing unbounded growth.

Log retention strategy:
- Definition files (`.claude/evals/<feature>.md`) are never auto-deleted
- Log files (`.claude/evals/<feature>.log`) are trimmed to last 10 runs
- Completed features can be manually archived after shipping

**Sources:** [commands/eval.md:113-120]()

