## NanoClaw REPL Pattern

### Architecture

A persistent REPL that wraps `claude -p` with session management. Unlike sequential pipelines, NanoClaw maintains conversation history across invocations using a Markdown file as the database.

**NanoClaw Session Management Flow**

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Start["User Input"] --> Load["Load Session:\n~/.claude/claw/{session}.md"]
    Load --> History["Build Prompt:\nHistory + New Message"]
    History --> Claude["claude -p\n(with full history)"]
    Claude --> Append["Append Response\nto Session File"]
    Append --> Prompt["Next User Input"]
    Prompt --> Load
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:104-132](), [scripts/claw.js:1-end]()

### Code Implementation

The NanoClaw REPL is implemented in [scripts/claw.js:1-end](). Key components:

**Session Loading**: [scripts/claw.js:1-end]() loads conversation history from `~/.claude/claw/{CLAW_SESSION}.md` on startup.

**Context Injection**: [scripts/claw.js:1-end]() builds the full prompt by concatenating:
1. Session history (all previous turns)
2. Skill context (if `CLAW_SKILLS` set)
3. New user message

**Response Persistence**: [scripts/claw.js:1-end]() appends Claude's response to the session file in Markdown format:
```markdown
## User
{message}

## Assistant
{response}
```

### Usage

```bash
# Start default session
node scripts/claw.js

# Named session with skill context
CLAW_SESSION=my-project CLAW_SKILLS=tdd-workflow,security-review node scripts/claw.js
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:107-114]()

### NanoClaw vs Sequential Pipeline

| Use Case | NanoClaw | Sequential Pipeline |
|----------|----------|-------------------|
| Interactive exploration | Yes | No |
| Scripted automation | No | Yes |
| Session persistence | Built-in | Manual |
| Context accumulation | Grows per turn | Fresh each step |
| CI/CD integration | Poor | Excellent |
| Max context window | Limited by history | Unlimited (fresh each step) |

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:123-132]()

