## Continuous Claude PR Loop Pattern

### Architecture

A production-grade shell script that runs Claude Code in a continuous loop: create branch, implement, create PR, wait for CI, auto-fix failures, merge, repeat. Context persists across iterations via `SHARED_TASK_NOTES.md`.

**Continuous Claude PR Loop Flow**

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start["Iteration N"] --> Branch["Create Branch:\ncontinuous-claude/iteration-N"]
    Branch --> Implement["claude -p with prompt"]
    Implement --> Review{"--review-prompt\nspecified?"}
    Review -->|Yes| ReviewPass["claude -p review pass"]
    Review -->|No| Commit["Commit Changes"]
    ReviewPass --> Commit
    Commit --> PR["gh pr create"]
    PR --> WaitCI["Poll: gh pr checks"]
    WaitCI --> CIResult{"CI Status"}
    CIResult -->|Fail| FixAttempt{"Retry count\n< max?"}
    FixAttempt -->|Yes| FixPass["claude -p fix pass\nwith CI logs"]
    FixPass --> WaitCI
    FixAttempt -->|No| Abort["Abort Iteration"]
    CIResult -->|Pass| Merge["Merge PR\n(squash/merge/rebase)"]
    Merge --> UpdateNotes["Update\nSHARED_TASK_NOTES.md"]
    UpdateNotes --> LimitCheck{"Max runs/cost/\nduration reached?"}
    LimitCheck -->|No| NextIter["Iteration N+1"]
    LimitCheck -->|Yes| Done["Loop Complete"]
    NextIter --> Branch
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:206-259]()

### Core Components

**Branch Creation**: Each iteration creates a new branch `continuous-claude/iteration-N` from main.

**Prompt Execution**: Runs `claude -p` with the user-specified prompt. Optional `--review-prompt` adds a separate reviewer pass.

**PR Creation**: Uses `gh pr create` to open PR with auto-generated title/body.

**CI Polling**: Polls `gh pr checks` until all checks complete or timeout.

**Auto-Fix on Failure**: If CI fails, spawns new `claude -p` with CI logs and fix context. Re-pushes and waits for checks again.

**Merge Strategy**: Configurable via `--merge-strategy`: `squash`, `merge`, or `rebase`.

**Context Bridge**: `SHARED_TASK_NOTES.md` file persists across iterations.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:213-228]()

### Installation and Usage

```bash
# Install
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AnandChowdhary/continuous-claude/HEAD/install.sh | bash

# Basic: 10 iterations
continuous-claude --prompt "Add unit tests for all untested functions" --max-runs 10

# Cost-limited
continuous-claude --prompt "Fix all linter errors" --max-cost 5.00

# Time-boxed
continuous-claude --prompt "Improve test coverage" --max-duration 8h

# With code review pass
continuous-claude \
  --prompt "Add authentication feature" \
  --max-runs 10 \
  --review-prompt "Run npm test && npm run lint, fix any failures"

# Parallel via worktrees
continuous-claude --prompt "Add tests" --max-runs 5 --worktree tests-worker &
continuous-claude --prompt "Refactor code" --max-runs 5 --worktree refactor-worker &
wait
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:238-259]()

### Cross-Iteration Context: SHARED_TASK_NOTES.md

The critical innovation for bridging context across independent `claude -p` invocations:

```markdown
## Progress
- [x] Added tests for auth module (iteration 1)
- [x] Fixed edge case in token refresh (iteration 2)
- [ ] Still need: rate limiting tests, error boundary tests

## Next Steps
- Focus on rate limiting module next
- The mock setup in tests/helpers.ts can be reused
```

Claude reads this file at iteration start and updates it at iteration end. This provides:
- **Task continuity**: What was accomplished in previous iterations
- **Failure context**: What didn't work and why
- **Next actions**: What to focus on next
- **Shared knowledge**: Reusable patterns discovered

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:261-275]()

### CI Failure Recovery

When PR checks fail, Continuous Claude automatically:

1. Fetches failed run ID via `gh run list`
2. Spawns new `claude -p` with CI fix context
3. Claude inspects logs via `gh run view`, fixes code, commits, pushes
4. Re-waits for checks (up to `--ci-retry-max` attempts)

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:277-285]()

### Completion Signal

Claude can signal "I'm done" by outputting a magic phrase:

```bash
continuous-claude \
  --prompt "Fix all bugs in the issue tracker" \
  --completion-signal "CONTINUOUS_CLAUDE_PROJECT_COMPLETE" \
  --completion-threshold 3  # Stops after 3 consecutive signals
```

Three consecutive iterations signaling completion stops the loop, preventing wasted runs on finished work.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:287-297]()

### Configuration Flags

| Flag | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `--max-runs N` | Stop after N successful iterations |
| `--max-cost $X` | Stop after spending $X (requires cost tracking) |
| `--max-duration 2h` | Stop after elapsed time |
| `--merge-strategy squash` | How to merge PRs: squash, merge, or rebase |
| `--worktree <name>` | Parallel execution via git worktrees |
| `--disable-commits` | Dry-run mode (no git operations) |
| `--review-prompt "..."` | Add reviewer pass per iteration |
| `--ci-retry-max N` | Auto-fix CI failures (default: 1) |
| `--completion-signal "..."` | Magic phrase to signal completion |
| `--completion-threshold N` | Consecutive signals to stop (default: 1) |

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:301-310]()

---

## De-Sloppify Pattern

### Problem Statement

LLMs implement TDD too literally, generating:
- Tests that verify TypeScript's type system works (`typeof x === 'string'`)
- Overly defensive runtime checks for type-guaranteed conditions
- Tests for framework behavior rather than business logic
- Excessive error handling that obscures actual code

Adding negative instructions ("don't test type systems") to the Implementer prompt causes it to become hesitant about ALL testing, skipping legitimate edge cases.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:315-325]()

### Solution: Separate Cleanup Pass

Instead of constraining the Implementer, let it be thorough. Then add a focused cleanup agent with its own context window:

```bash
# Step 1: Implement (let it be thorough)
claude -p "Implement the feature with full TDD. Be thorough with tests."

# Step 2: De-sloppify (separate context, focused cleanup)
claude -p "Review all changes in the working tree. Remove:
- Tests that verify language/framework behavior rather than business logic
- Redundant type checks that the type system already enforces
- Over-defensive error handling for impossible states
- Console.log statements
- Commented-out code

Keep all business logic tests. Run the test suite after cleanup to ensure nothing breaks."
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:333-349]()

### Integration into Loops

```bash
for feature in "${features[@]}"; do
  # Implement
  claude -p "Implement $feature with TDD."

  # De-sloppify
  claude -p "Cleanup pass: review changes, remove test/code slop, run tests."

  # Verify
  claude -p "Run build + lint + tests. Fix any failures."

  # Commit
  claude -p "Commit with message: feat: add $feature"
done
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:353-367]()

### Key Insight

> Rather than adding negative instructions which have downstream quality effects, add a separate de-sloppify pass. Two focused agents outperform one constrained agent.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:369-373]()

