## Decision Framework

### Choosing the Right Pattern

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start["Need Autonomous Loop?"] --> Single{"Single focused\nchange?"}
    Single -->|Yes| Simple["Sequential Pipeline\nor NanoClaw REPL"]
    Single -->|No| Spec{"Written spec/RFC\nexists?"}
    Spec -->|No| Variations{"Many variations\nof same thing?"}
    Variations -->|Yes| Infinite["Infinite Agentic Loop"]
    Variations -->|No| SimpleFallback["Sequential Pipeline\nwith De-Sloppify"]
    Spec -->|Yes| Parallel{"Need parallel\nimplementation?"}
    Parallel -->|No| Continuous["Continuous Claude\nPR Loop"]
    Parallel -->|Yes| Ralphinho["Ralphinho\nDAG Orchestration"]
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:543-558]()

### When to Use Each Pattern

| Signal | Use Ralphinho | Use Continuous Claude | Use Sequential Pipeline | Use Infinite Loop |
|--------|--------------|---------------------|----------------------|------------------|
| Multiple interdependent work units | Yes | No | No | No |
| Need parallel implementation | Yes | Via worktrees | No | Yes |
| Merge conflicts likely | Yes | No | No | No |
| Single-file change | No | Maybe | Yes | No |
| Multi-day project | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| Spec/RFC already written | Yes | Maybe | No | Required |
| Quick iteration on one thing | No | No | Yes | No |
| Content generation (N variations) | No | No | No | Yes |

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:531-541]()

---

## Combining Patterns

These patterns compose well together:

### Sequential Pipeline + De-Sloppify

The most common combination. Every implement step gets a cleanup pass:

```bash
claude -p "Implement feature with TDD"
claude -p "De-sloppify: remove test/code slop"
claude -p "Run verify"
claude -p "Commit"
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:560-565]()

### Continuous Claude + De-Sloppify

Add `--review-prompt` with a de-sloppify directive to each iteration:

```bash
continuous-claude \
  --prompt "Implement features" \
  --review-prompt "De-sloppify pass: remove unnecessary tests and checks, run test suite" \
  --max-runs 10
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:560-567]()

### Any Loop + Verification

Use ECC's `/verify` command or `verification-loop` skill as a gate before commits. This integrates the verification pattern from [Checkpoint & Verification Workflow](#10.5) into any loop:

```bash
claude -p "Implement feature"
claude -p "Run /verify command"  # Build → Type → Lint → Test → Git
claude -p "Commit if verify passed"
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:560-569]()

### Ralphinho's Tiered Approach in Simpler Loops

Even in a sequential pipeline, route simple tasks to Haiku and complex tasks to Opus:

```bash
# Simple formatting fix
claude -p --model haiku "Fix the import ordering in src/utils.ts"

# Complex architectural change
claude -p --model opus "Refactor the auth module to use the strategy pattern"
```

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:571-578]()

---

## Anti-Patterns

### Common Mistakes

**Infinite loops without exit conditions**: Always have a max-runs, max-cost, max-duration, or completion signal. Without these, loops consume resources indefinitely.

**No context bridge between iterations**: Each `claude -p` call starts with a fresh context window. Use `SHARED_TASK_NOTES.md` (Continuous Claude), session files (NanoClaw), or filesystem state (Sequential Pipeline) to bridge context.

**Retrying the same failure**: If an iteration fails, don't just retry with the same prompt. Capture the error context (logs, diffs, test output) and feed it to the next attempt. See Ralphinho's eviction recovery pattern.

**Negative instructions instead of cleanup passes**: Don't say "don't do X" in the Implementer prompt. Add a separate pass that removes X. See [De-Sloppify Pattern](#de-sloppify-pattern).

**All agents in one context window**: For complex workflows, separate concerns into different agent processes. The reviewer should never be the author. See Ralphinho's separate-context architecture.

**Ignoring file overlap in parallel work**: If two parallel agents might edit the same file, you need a merge strategy (sequential landing, rebase, or conflict resolution). See Ralphinho's merge queue with file overlap intelligence.

Sources: [skills/autonomous-loops/SKILL.md:582-596]()

