## Agent Delegation & Orchestration

The system implements agent orchestration where the main Claude instance delegates specialized tasks to bounded subagents. This reduces context pressure and enables parallel execution. The `chief-of-staff` agent enforces quality through PostToolUse hooks that run after every tool execution.

### Bounded Contexts and Tool Sandboxing

Each agent operates within a bounded context defined by:
- **Tool Permissions**: Restricted subset of available tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)
- **Skill Access**: Pre-loaded skills relevant to the agent's domain
- **Model Selection**: Appropriate model tier (Haiku for simple tasks, Sonnet for moderate, Opus for complex)
- **Execution Mode**: Foreground (blocking) or background (non-blocking)

**Agent Definition Structure**

Agents are defined in markdown files with YAML front matter:

```yaml
---
name: security-reviewer
description: Security vulnerability analysis and secret detection
model: claude-sonnet-4
tools: [Read, Grep, Glob]
skills: [security-review]
---
```

The agent markdown then contains the specialized instructions and workflows.

**Tool Sandboxing by Agent Type**

| Agent Type | Read | Write | Edit | Bash | Grep | Glob | Rationale |
|------------|------|-------|------|------|------|------|-----------|
| planner | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | Read-only analysis |
| architect | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | Read-only design |
| code-reviewer | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Analysis + linting |
| security-reviewer | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Scanning only |
| tdd-guide | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Full test creation |
| e2e-runner | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Test execution |
| build-error-resolver | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Fix compilation |
| refactor-cleaner | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | Safe refactoring |
| doc-updater | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | Documentation only |

**Architecture: Agent Delegation Flow**

```mermaid
graph TB
    User["User Request"] --> Orchestrator["Main Claude Orchestrator<br/>(Opus 4.5)"]
    
    Orchestrator --> Decision{"Task Type?"}
    
    Decision -->|"Planning"| Planner["planner agent<br/>model: sonnet-4<br/>tools: [Read, Grep, Glob]"]
    Decision -->|"Testing"| TDD["tdd-guide agent<br/>model: opus-4<br/>tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]"]
    Decision -->|"Security"| Security["security-reviewer agent<br/>model: sonnet-4<br/>tools: [Read, Grep, Glob, Bash]"]
    Decision -->|"Documentation"| Doc["doc-updater agent<br/>model: haiku<br/>tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Grep, Glob]"]
    
    Planner --> SkillLoader["Skill Loader"]
    TDD --> SkillLoader
    Security --> SkillLoader
    Doc --> SkillLoader
    
    SkillLoader --> PlannerSkills["Load: backend-patterns"]
    SkillLoader --> TDDSkills["Load: tdd-workflow<br/>eval-harness"]
    SkillLoader --> SecuritySkills["Load: security-review"]
    SkillLoader --> DocSkills["Load: [none]"]
    
    Planner --> PlannerExec["Execute in Foreground<br/>Return: Feature Plan"]
    TDD --> TDDExec["Execute in Foreground<br/>Return: Test Suite + Implementation"]
    Security --> SecurityExec["Execute in Background<br/>Return: Security Report"]
    Doc --> DocExec["Execute in Background<br/>Return: Updated Docs"]
    
    PlannerExec --> Orchestrator
    TDDExec --> Orchestrator
    SecurityExec --> Orchestrator
    DocExec --> Orchestrator
```

Sources: [examples/user-CLAUDE.md:14-21](), [the-shortform-guide.md:76-96]()

### Foreground vs Background Execution

**Foreground Execution**
- Blocks main orchestrator until completion
- Used for tasks that block subsequent work (planning, implementation)
- Results directly inform next steps
- Example: `/tdd` command runs tdd-guide agent in foreground

**Background Execution**
- Non-blocking, runs in parallel with main thread
- Used for tasks that don't affect immediate workflow (linting, documentation)
- Results reported asynchronously
- Example: Observer agent always runs in background

**Parallel Execution with Task Tool**

The main orchestrator can spawn multiple agents in parallel using the Task tool:

```markdown
Task 1: Run security-reviewer on auth module
Task 2: Run code-reviewer on API routes
Task 3: Run doc-updater on README

[All three execute simultaneously]
```

This pattern is particularly effective when tasks operate on non-overlapping files or domains.

Sources: [examples/user-CLAUDE.md:18-19](), [the-shortform-guide.md:199-207]()

