## Agent Definition Format

Each agent is defined in a Markdown file with YAML front matter specifying:

| Field | Description | Example |
|-------|-------------|---------|
| `name` | Agent identifier | `code-reviewer` |
| `description` | Agent purpose and scope | `Code review for quality/security` |
| `model` | Claude model to use | `claude-opus-4.5`, `claude-sonnet-4` |
| `tools` | Allowed tools (sandboxing) | `["Read", "Grep", "Glob"]` |
| `mcps` | Allowed MCP servers | `["github", "memory"]` |
| `skills` | Available skills | `["backend-patterns", "security-review"]` |

Example agent structure:

```markdown
---
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for quality, security, and best practices
model: claude-sonnet-4
tools: [Read, Grep, Glob]
skills: [backend-patterns, security-review, coding-standards]
---

# Code Reviewer Agent

## Role
You are a specialized code reviewer focusing on...

## Responsibilities
1. Check code quality
2. Identify security vulnerabilities
3. Verify best practices

## Review Criteria
...
```

**Sources:** [the-shortform-guide.md:82-95](), [examples/user-CLAUDE.md:44-56]()

## Orchestrator Agents vs Specialist Agents

The system distinguishes between two agent types:

### Orchestrator Agents

Orchestrator agents coordinate complex workflows and delegate to specialist agents:

| Agent | Model | Tools | Role |
|-------|-------|-------|------|
| `planner` | Sonnet | Read, Grep, Glob | Breaks down features, delegates to `architect` |
| `orchestrator` | Opus | All tools | Sequential workflow coordination with handoff docs |

**Characteristics:**
- Have broader tool permissions
- Coordinate multi-phase workflows
- Create handoff documents between phases
- Aggregate results from specialist agents
- Use higher-tier models (Sonnet/Opus)

### Specialist Agents

Specialist agents perform focused tasks within limited scope:

| Agent | Model | Tools | Scope |
|-------|-------|-------|-------|
| `architect` | Opus | Read, Bash | System design (read-only) |
| `code-reviewer` | Opus | Read, Grep | Code quality analysis (read-only) |
| `tdd-guide` | Sonnet | Edit, Bash, Grep | Test implementation |
| `security-reviewer` | Opus | Read, Bash | Security scanning (read-only) |
| `go-reviewer` | Sonnet | Read, Grep | Go code review (read-only) |

**Characteristics:**
- Limited tool permissions
- Single responsibility
- Read-only or write-within-scope
- Use task-appropriate models
- Return structured outputs

### Tool Permission Boundaries

**Delegation Pattern with Tool Restrictions**

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Orchestrator Layer"
        MainClaude["Main Claude<br/>All tools enabled"]
        Planner["planner<br/>Read, Grep, Glob<br/>(Sonnet)"]
        Orchestrator["orchestrator<br/>All tools<br/>(Opus)"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Read-Only Specialist Agents"
        Architect["architect<br/>Read, Bash<br/>No Write/Edit"]
        CodeReviewer["code-reviewer<br/>Read, Grep<br/>No Write/Edit/Bash"]
        SecurityReviewer["security-reviewer<br/>Read, Bash<br/>No Write/Edit"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Write-Enabled Specialist Agents"
        TDDGuide["tdd-guide<br/>Edit, Bash, Grep<br/>Can modify code"]
        BuildResolver["build-error-resolver<br/>Edit, Bash, Grep<br/>Can fix builds"]
    end
    
    MainClaude -->|"delegates with<br/>reduced permissions"| Planner
    MainClaude -->|"delegates with<br/>full permissions"| Orchestrator
    
    Planner -->|"delegates<br/>read-only"| Architect
    Orchestrator -->|"sequential<br/>handoff docs"| Architect
    Orchestrator -->|"sequential<br/>handoff docs"| TDDGuide
    Orchestrator -->|"sequential<br/>handoff docs"| CodeReviewer
    
    Architect -.->|"blocked"| Write["Write tool"]
    Architect -.->|"blocked"| Edit["Edit tool"]
    CodeReviewer -.->|"blocked"| Bash["Bash tool"]
```

**Tool Access Matrix**

| Tool | Orchestrators | Read-Only Specialists | Write-Enabled Specialists |
|------|--------------|----------------------|--------------------------|
| Read | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Grep | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Glob | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Write | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| Edit | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| Bash | ✅ | Varies | ✅ |

**Design Rationale:**

- **Read-only agents** cannot modify code, ensuring safe analysis
- **Write-enabled agents** can modify files but only within their scope
- **Bash access** varies: `architect` can run commands, `code-reviewer` cannot
- **No agent has unrestricted access**: Main orchestrator retains full control

This sandboxing prevents:
- Accidental modifications outside agent domain
- Destructive commands beyond scope
- Unauthorized access to sensitive data

**Sources:** [README.md:149-160](), [the-shortform-guide.md:76-95](), [commands/orchestrate.md:1-173]()

## Bounded Contexts and Tool Sandboxing

Each agent operates within a **bounded context** defined by:

1. **Tool permissions**: Restricted subset of available tools (see Tool Access Matrix above)
2. **MCP access**: Limited to specific integrations
3. **Skill access**: Only relevant knowledge bases
4. **Scope definition**: Explicit responsibilities in agent definition

Example agent definition showing bounded context:

```yaml
---
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for quality, security, and best practices
model: claude-opus-4.5
tools: [Read, Grep, Glob]  # Read-only, cannot modify code
mcps: [github, memory]     # Limited MCP access
skills: [backend-patterns, security-review, coding-standards]
---
```

The `code-reviewer` agent:
- Can read and search files (Read, Grep, Glob)
- Cannot modify code (no Write/Edit)
- Cannot execute commands (no Bash)
- Only accesses GitHub and memory MCPs
- Uses 3 relevant skills

**Sources:** [README.md:149-160](), [agents/code-reviewer.md:1-15](), [the-shortform-guide.md:76-95]()

## Execution Modes

Agents support two execution modes controlled by the main orchestrator:

### Foreground Execution

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant MainClaude as "Main Claude"
    participant Agent as "Subagent"
    participant Tools
    
    User->>MainClaude: "/code-review src/api"
    MainClaude->>Agent: Delegate review task (blocking)
    Agent->>Tools: Read(src/api/*.ts)
    Tools-->>Agent: File contents
    Agent->>Tools: Grep(security patterns)
    Tools-->>Agent: Findings
    Agent->>Agent: Analyze code
    Agent-->>MainClaude: Review report
    MainClaude-->>User: Display report
    
    Note over MainClaude: Blocked during agent execution
```

**Characteristics:**
- Main orchestrator waits for agent completion
- Used for tasks requiring immediate results
- Agent output directly shown to user
- Example: `/code-review`, `/go-review`, `/build-fix`

### Background Execution

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User
    participant MainClaude as "Main Claude"
    participant Observer as "observer agent"
    participant Storage as "~/.claude/"
    
    User->>MainClaude: Edit files, run commands
    MainClaude->>Storage: Write observations.jsonl
    MainClaude-->>User: Continue conversation
    
    Note over Observer: Runs every 5 minutes
    Observer->>Storage: Check observations.jsonl
    Storage-->>Observer: 10+ lines
    Observer->>Observer: Analyze patterns (Haiku model)
    Observer->>Storage: Write instincts/*.yaml
    Observer->>Storage: Archive observations
    
    Note over MainClaude: Not blocked, continues working
```

**Characteristics:**
- Main orchestrator continues without waiting
- Used for non-critical, long-running tasks
- Agent operates independently on schedule
- Example: Observer agent pattern learning

**Sources:** [the-shortform-guide.md:76-79]()

## Parallel Execution with Task Tool

The `Task` tool enables parallel agent execution, allowing multiple subagents to work simultaneously:

```mermaid
graph TB
    User["User: Implement feature X"]
    MainClaude["Main Claude Orchestrator"]
    
    subgraph ParallelExecution["Parallel Execution via Task Tool"]
        Task1["Task 1: planner agent<br/>Break down feature"]
        Task2["Task 2: architect agent<br/>Design system"]
        Task3["Task 3: security-reviewer agent<br/>Identify risks"]
    end
    
    Results["Aggregate Results"]
    Implementation["Main Claude: Implement based on results"]
    
    User --> MainClaude
    MainClaude --> Task1
    MainClaude --> Task2
    MainClaude --> Task3
    
    Task1 --> Results
    Task2 --> Results
    Task3 --> Results
    
    Results --> Implementation
    
    Note1["All 3 agents run<br/>simultaneously"]
    Note2["Each has isolated context<br/>and tool permissions"]
    
    Task1 -.-> Note1
    Task1 -.-> Note2
```

### Parallel Execution Pattern

From [examples/user-CLAUDE.md:18]():

```markdown
**Parallel Execution**: Use Task tool with multiple agents when possible
```

**Implementation example:**

```markdown
# User request: Add authentication feature

# Main Claude orchestrates:
Task 1: planner agent - Break down authentication requirements
Task 2: security-reviewer agent - Identify security considerations  
Task 3: database-reviewer agent - Design user schema

# All three execute simultaneously, each with:
- Isolated context window
- Bounded tool access
- Specific skill sets
- Different models (cost optimization)

# Main Claude aggregates results and implements
```

### Benefits of Parallel Execution

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Speed** | 3x faster when 3 agents run in parallel |
| **Context optimization** | Each agent uses separate context window |
| **Cost efficiency** | Use Haiku for simple tasks, Opus for complex ones |
| **Specialization** | Each agent focuses on its domain |
| **Independence** | No blocking between non-overlapping tasks |

**Limitations:**

- Tasks must be non-overlapping (no file conflicts)
- Limited to Task tool availability
- Requires careful orchestration to aggregate results

**Sources:** [examples/user-CLAUDE.md:18](), [the-shortform-guide.md:202-207]()

