## Purpose and Scope

This document covers strategies for minimizing token consumption and associated API costs in Claude Code. While [Context Window Management](#10.3) focuses on configuration to preserve context space, this page addresses the economics of token usage: model selection hierarchies, tool choice tradeoffs, and architectural patterns that reduce token burn.

For agent delegation patterns that distribute work across cheaper models, see [Agent Delegation & Orchestration](#12.1). For MCP configuration details, see [MCP Server Configuration](#10.4).
</thinking>

## Core Token Optimization Dimensions

Token optimization operates across four interconnected dimensions, each with measurable impact on cost and performance:

```mermaid
graph TB
    TokenCost["Token Cost Optimization"]
    
    TokenCost --> ModelSelection["1. Model Selection<br/>Haiku→Sonnet→Opus"]
    TokenCost --> ToolChoice["2. Tool Choice<br/>mgrep vs grep<br/>CLI vs MCP"]
    TokenCost --> ContextMgmt["3. Context Management<br/>Selective MCP loading"]
    TokenCost --> CodeArch["4. Code Architecture<br/>Modular vs monolithic"]
    
    ModelSelection --> Cost1["$0.80 → $15.00/MTok<br/>18.75x difference"]
    ToolChoice --> Cost2["~50% token reduction<br/>mgrep benchmark"]
    ContextMgmt --> Cost3["200k → 70k degradation<br/>with excessive MCPs"]
    CodeArch --> Cost4["Higher success rate<br/>fewer retries"]
    
    Cost1 --> Total["Combined Effect:<br/>10-50x cost variance"]
    Cost2 --> Total
    Cost3 --> Total
    Cost4 --> Total
```

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:104-145](), [README.md:53-60]()

## Model Selection Strategy

The primary token optimization lever is model selection. Claude offers three models with vastly different pricing and capabilities:

### Pricing Matrix

| Model | Input Cost | Output Cost | Use Cases | Token Efficiency |
|-------|-----------|-------------|-----------|------------------|
| Haiku | $0.80/MTok | $4.00/MTok | Exploration, simple edits, documentation | Baseline |
| Sonnet | $3.00/MTok | $15.00/MTok | Multi-file coding, standard tasks | 3.75x Haiku |
| Opus | $15.00/MTok | $75.00/MTok | Architecture, security, deep reasoning | 18.75x Haiku |

**Key Insight:** The 18.75x cost difference between Haiku and Opus means strategic model selection can reduce costs by an order of magnitude while maintaining quality.

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:129-133]()

### Hierarchical Model Selection

The optimal strategy is hierarchical fallback: start with the cheapest sufficient model, upgrade on failure.

```mermaid
graph TB
    Task["Development Task"]
    
    Task --> Simple{"Simple?<br/>Single file<br/>Clear spec"}
    Task --> Standard{"Standard?<br/>Multi-file<br/>Common patterns"}
    Task --> Complex{"Complex?<br/>Architecture<br/>Security critical"}
    
    Simple -->|Yes| Haiku["Haiku Agent<br/>$0.80/MTok input<br/>Fast exploration"]
    Standard -->|Yes| Sonnet["Sonnet Agent<br/>$3.00/MTok input<br/>Default for 90% tasks"]
    Complex -->|Yes| Opus["Opus Agent<br/>$15.00/MTok input<br/>Deep reasoning"]
    
    Haiku -->|Failure| Sonnet
    Sonnet -->|Failure| Opus
    
    Haiku --> Success["Task Complete"]
    Sonnet --> Success
    Opus --> Success
```

**Agent Configuration in Practice:**

The repository's agent definitions demonstrate model selection in their YAML frontmatter:

```
agents/
├── planner.md              # model: sonnet (planning workflow)
├── architect.md            # model: opus (system design)
├── tdd-guide.md           # model: sonnet (test implementation)
├── code-reviewer.md       # model: opus (quality/security review)
├── security-reviewer.md   # model: opus (vulnerability analysis)
├── build-error-resolver.md # model: sonnet (error fixes)
├── e2e-runner.md          # model: sonnet (test execution)
├── refactor-cleaner.md    # model: haiku (cleanup tasks)
└── doc-updater.md         # model: haiku (documentation sync)
```

Example agent configurations:

[agents/code-reviewer.md:1-10]() - Opus for critical review:
```markdown
---
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for quality, security, and maintainability
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob", "Bash"]
model: opus
---
```

[agents/refactor-cleaner.md:1-10]() - Haiku for cleanup:
```markdown
---
name: refactor-cleaner
description: Remove dead code and unused files
tools: ["Read", "Edit", "Bash"]
model: haiku
---
```

Sources: [README.md:216-230](), [the-longform-guide.md:110-126]()

### Task-to-Model Mapping

| Task Category | Recommended Model | Rationale |
|--------------|------------------|-----------|
| File exploration | Haiku | Fast, cheap, good enough for navigation |
| Single-file edits | Haiku | Clear instructions, limited scope |
| Documentation writing | Haiku | Structure is straightforward |
| Multi-file implementation | Sonnet | Best balance for coding tasks |
| PR reviews | Sonnet | Understands context, catches nuance |
| Refactoring | Sonnet | Medium complexity, needs broad understanding |
| Architecture decisions | Opus | Deep reasoning, system-wide thinking |
| Security analysis | Opus | Can't afford to miss vulnerabilities |
| Complex bug investigation | Opus | Must hold entire system in mind |

**Default Strategy:** Use Sonnet for 90% of coding tasks. Upgrade to Opus when:
- First attempt with Sonnet failed
- Task spans 5+ files
- Architectural decisions required
- Security-critical code
- Deep system understanding needed

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:116-125]()

## Tool Optimization: mgrep vs grep

Tool choice significantly impacts token consumption. The `mgrep` plugin demonstrates measurable token reduction compared to traditional search tools.

### mgrep Benchmark Results

In a 50-task benchmark comparing `mgrep` + Claude Code vs `grep`-based workflows:

```mermaid
graph LR
    Benchmark["50-Task Benchmark"]
    
    Benchmark --> Grep["grep/ripgrep Workflow"]
    Benchmark --> Mgrep["mgrep Workflow"]
    
    Grep --> GrepTokens["~2.0x baseline tokens"]
    Grep --> GrepQuality["Baseline quality"]
    
    Mgrep --> MgrepTokens["~1.0x baseline tokens<br/>~50% reduction"]
    Mgrep --> MgrepQuality["Similar or better quality"]
    
    GrepTokens --> Savings["Token Savings:<br/>~50% per search"]
    MgrepTokens --> Savings
```

**Why mgrep Is More Efficient:**

Traditional grep returns large result sets with full file paths, line numbers, and surrounding context. The output format is verbose:

```
./src/components/Header.tsx:42:  const handleSubmit = async () => {
./src/components/Header.tsx:43:    // submit logic
./src/utils/api.ts:156:  const handleSubmit = async (data) => {
./src/utils/api.ts:157:    return fetch('/api/submit', {...})
./tests/api.test.ts:89:  it('should call handleSubmit', () => {
```

mgrep returns structured, deduplicated results optimized for LLM consumption:

```
handleSubmit found in:
- src/components/Header.tsx (line 42)
- src/utils/api.ts (line 156)  
- tests/api.test.ts (line 89)
```

**Token Impact:**
- grep output: ~150-200 tokens per result
- mgrep output: ~50-80 tokens per result
- Average savings: **50-60% per search operation**

**Installation:**

```bash
# Add marketplace
/plugin marketplace add https://github.com/mixedbread-ai/mgrep

# Install plugin
/plugin install mgrep@Mixedbread-Grep
```

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:135-139](), [the-shortform-guide.md:222-228](), [README.md:53-60]()

## MCP vs CLI Tradeoffs

MCPs provide convenient wrappers around external services but consume significant token overhead. For many services, using CLI tools directly via skills offers better token efficiency.

### MCP Token Overhead

Each enabled MCP contributes tool definitions that occupy context space before any conversation begins:

```mermaid
graph TB
    MCP["MCP Server<br/>(e.g., GitHub)"]
    
    MCP --> Tools["8-12 Tool Definitions"]
    
    Tools --> T1["list_repos<br/>~600 tokens"]
    Tools --> T2["create_issue<br/>~800 tokens"]
    Tools --> T3["search_code<br/>~700 tokens"]
    Tools --> T4["get_pr<br/>~600 tokens"]
    Tools --> T5["create_pr<br/>~900 tokens"]
    Tools --> T6["...more tools<br/>~2400 tokens"]
    
    T1 --> Total["Total Overhead:<br/>~6000 tokens"]
    T2 --> Total
    T3 --> Total
    T4 --> Total
    T5 --> Total
    T6 --> Total
    
    Total --> ContextImpact["Context Impact:<br/>Always loaded<br/>Whether used or not"]
```

### CLI Alternative Pattern

For services with robust CLIs, wrap CLI commands in skills instead of using MCPs. The repository demonstrates this with GitHub operations.

**MCP Approach (6000 token overhead):**
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"]
    }
  }
}
```

**CLI + Command Approach (~200 token overhead):**

The repository provides command wrappers that use `gh` CLI directly. For example, PR creation hooks use CLI tools:

[hooks/hooks.json:1-50]() references `gh-pr-create-hook` which executes:
```bash
gh pr create --title "$TITLE" --body "$BODY"
```

Commands can invoke these patterns:
```markdown
---
name: create-pr
description: Create GitHub PR using gh CLI
---

1. Verify authentication: `gh auth status`
2. Create PR: `gh pr create`
3. Link to issue: `gh pr create --issue <number>`
```

**Package Manager Detection Pattern:**

[scripts/lib/package-manager.js:1-200]() demonstrates CLI wrapping for package managers:

```javascript
// Detects package manager without MCP overhead
function detectPackageManager() {
  // Priority cascade: env var → project config → lock files
  return process.env.CLAUDE_PACKAGE_MANAGER 
    || readProjectConfig()
    || detectFromLockFile()
    || 'npm';
}

function generateCommands(pm) {
  // Returns CLI command strings, not MCP tools
  return {
    installCmd: `${pm} install`,
    runCmd: `${pm} run`,
    addCmd: `${pm} add`,
    // ...
  };
}
```

This pattern is invoked by [scripts/hooks/session-start.js:1-100]() to inject package manager commands into session context without MCP overhead.

Sources: [scripts/lib/package-manager.js:1-200](), [README.md:176-203]()

**Token Comparison:**

| Approach | Overhead | When Invoked | Total Cost |
|----------|----------|--------------|------------|
| MCP (always loaded) | 6000 tokens | 0 tokens | 6000 tokens |
| CLI + Skill | 200 tokens | 0 tokens | 200 tokens |
| CLI + Skill + Command | 200 tokens | 150 tokens | 350 tokens |

**Savings: ~94% token reduction** (6000 → 350 tokens)

### When to Use MCPs vs CLI

| Scenario | Recommended Approach | Rationale |
|----------|---------------------|-----------|
| Frequent, complex queries | MCP | Convenience worth overhead |
| Occasional operations | CLI + Skill | Token savings significant |
| Read-only operations | CLI + Skill | Simple wrappers sufficient |
| Authenticated operations | MCP | Auth handling built-in |
| Batch operations | CLI + Skill | Scripts more efficient |

**Service-Specific Recommendations:**

| Service | MCP Configuration | CLI Alternative | Recommendation |
|---------|------------------|-----------------|----------------|
| GitHub | `@modelcontextprotocol/server-github` | `gh` CLI + commands | CLI for standard ops |
| Supabase | `@supabase/mcp-server-supabase` | `supabase` CLI + skills | MCP for SQL queries |
| Vercel | HTTP: `https://mcp.vercel.com` | `vercel` CLI | CLI for deploys |
| Railway | `@railway/mcp-server` | `railway` CLI | CLI for logs |
| ClickHouse | HTTP: `https://mcp.clickhouse.cloud/mcp` | `clickhouse-client` | MCP for analytics |

The repository's [mcp-configs/mcp-servers.json:1-50]() shows MCP definitions, while [hooks/hooks.json:50-100]() demonstrates CLI integration patterns in PostToolUse hooks.

Sources: [README.md:311-340](), [the-longform-guide.md:24-33](), [mcp-configs/mcp-servers.json:1-50]()

## Code Architecture Impact

File structure and modularity directly impact token consumption and success rates. Modular codebases consume fewer tokens and require fewer retry attempts.

### Monolithic vs Modular Files

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Monolithic Architecture"
        Mono["Single File<br/>2000+ lines"]
        Mono --> MonoRead["Read Tool:<br/>~3000 tokens"]
        Mono --> MonoEdit["Edit Operations:<br/>High failure rate"]
        Mono --> MonoRetry["Multiple Retries:<br/>~9000 tokens total"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Modular Architecture"
        Mod1["File 1<br/>200 lines"]
        Mod2["File 2<br/>200 lines"]
        Mod3["File 3<br/>200 lines"]
        
        Mod1 --> Mod1Read["Read: ~300 tokens"]
        Mod2 --> Mod2Read["Read: ~300 tokens"]
        Mod3 --> Mod3Read["Read: ~300 tokens"]
        
        Mod1Read --> ModSuccess["Edit Success:<br/>First attempt"]
        Mod2Read --> ModSuccess
        Mod3Read --> ModSuccess
        
        ModSuccess --> ModTotal["Total: ~900 tokens"]
    end
    
    MonoRetry --> Compare["10x token difference"]
    ModTotal --> Compare
```

### Token Cost by File Size

| File Size | Typical Tokens | Success Rate | Avg Retries | Total Token Cost |
|-----------|---------------|--------------|-------------|------------------|
| 100-300 lines | ~500 | 85% | 0.2 | ~600 tokens |
| 300-600 lines | ~1200 | 70% | 0.5 | ~1800 tokens |
| 600-1000 lines | ~2000 | 55% | 1.0 | ~4000 tokens |
| 1000-2000 lines | ~3500 | 35% | 2.5 | ~12,250 tokens |
| 2000+ lines | ~6000+ | 20% | 4.0+ | ~30,000+ tokens |

**Key Insight:** A 2000-line file costs **50x more tokens** than five 200-line files when accounting for retry attempts.

### Modular Architecture Benefits

**Token Efficiency:**
- Smaller read operations (fewer tokens per file access)
- Higher edit success rates (fewer retries)
- Easier context targeting (load only relevant files)

**Performance Benefits:**
- Faster initial reads
- More precise edits
- Reduced compaction frequency
- Better caching effectiveness

**Best Practices:**
- Target 100-300 lines per file
- Extract reusable functions to separate modules
- Use barrel exports for clean imports
- Split by feature, not by file type

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:142-144]()

## Context Window Economics

While [Context Window Management](#10.3) covers configuration, the economic impact of context degradation on token costs is significant.

### Context Degradation Impact

Excessive MCP loading degrades usable context, forcing more frequent compaction and context reconstruction:

```mermaid
graph LR
    Scenario1["Scenario A:<br/>5 MCPs enabled<br/>~130k usable context"]
    Scenario2["Scenario B:<br/>15 MCPs enabled<br/>~70k usable context"]
    
    Scenario1 --> Session1["Session Duration:<br/>~120 tool calls<br/>before compaction"]
    Scenario2 --> Session2["Session Duration:<br/>~60 tool calls<br/>before compaction"]
    
    Session1 --> Cost1["Compactions:<br/>2 per session<br/>~10k tokens each"]
    Session2 --> Cost2["Compactions:<br/>5 per session<br/>~10k tokens each"]
    
    Cost1 --> Total1["Total overhead:<br/>~20k tokens"]
    Cost2 --> Total2["Total overhead:<br/>~50k tokens"]
    
    Total1 --> Savings["2.5x token savings<br/>from MCP discipline"]
    Total2 --> Savings
```

**Compaction Overhead:**
- Each compaction: ~10,000 tokens
- Session with 130k context: ~2 compactions
- Session with 70k context: ~5 compactions
- **Additional cost: ~30,000 tokens** per session

### The 10/80 Rule

**Guideline:** Keep under 10 enabled MCPs and under 80 total active tools.

| Configuration | MCPs | Tools | Usable Context | Compaction Frequency | Session Token Cost |
|---------------|------|-------|----------------|---------------------|-------------------|
| Optimal | 5-6 | 40-60 | ~130k | Low (2-3x) | Baseline |
| Acceptable | 7-10 | 60-80 | ~110k | Moderate (3-4x) | +30% |
| Degraded | 11-15 | 80-120 | ~90k | High (4-6x) | +80% |
| Critical | 15+ | 120+ | ~70k | Critical (6-8x) | +150% |

**Token Cost Impact Example:**

A typical session with standard coding tasks:
- Optimal config (5 MCPs): ~50,000 input tokens
- Degraded config (15 MCPs): ~125,000 input tokens
- **Cost difference:** $0.15 vs $0.38 per session (2.5x)

Over 100 sessions: **$15 vs $38** in input costs alone.

Sources: [README.md:511-519](), [the-shortform-guide.md:138-153]()

## Practical Optimization Workflows

### Agent-Based Cost Optimization

Delegate work to specialized agents with model selection optimized for task complexity:

```mermaid
graph TB
    User["User Request:<br/>Implement feature"]
    
    User --> Explorer["1. Explore Agent<br/>Model: Haiku<br/>Task: Find relevant files"]
    Explorer --> Planner["2. Planner Agent<br/>Model: Sonnet<br/>Task: Create plan"]
    Planner --> Implementer["3. TDD Guide Agent<br/>Model: Sonnet<br/>Task: Write tests + code"]
    Implementer --> Reviewer["4. Code Reviewer Agent<br/>Model: Opus<br/>Task: Quality review"]
    
    Explorer --> Cost1["~$0.01<br/>~10k tokens"]
    Planner --> Cost2["~$0.05<br/>~15k tokens"]
    Implementer --> Cost3["~$0.30<br/>~100k tokens"]
    Reviewer --> Cost4["~$0.15<br/>~10k tokens"]
    
    Cost1 --> Total["Total: ~$0.51"]
    Cost2 --> Total
    Cost3 --> Total
    Cost4 --> Total
```

**Cost Breakdown:**
- Exploration (Haiku): ~10,000 tokens × $0.80/MTok = $0.01
- Planning (Sonnet): ~15,000 tokens × $3.00/MTok = $0.05
- Implementation (Sonnet): ~100,000 tokens × $3.00/MTok = $0.30
- Review (Opus): ~10,000 tokens × $15.00/MTok = $0.15
- **Total: $0.51 per feature**

**Alternative (all Opus):**
- Same workflow, all Opus: ~135,000 tokens × $15.00/MTok = **$2.03**
- **Savings: 75% cost reduction**

Sources: [the-longform-guide.md:107-126](), [agents/planner.md:1-10](), [agents/tdd-guide.md:1-10]()

### Tool Optimization Checklist

Systematic approach to reducing tool-related token costs:

| Optimization | Action | Token Savings | Implementation Effort |
|--------------|--------|---------------|---------------------|
| Replace grep | Install mgrep plugin | ~50% per search | Low |
| Disable unused MCPs | Use disabledMcpServers | ~6k per MCP | Low |
| CLI over MCP | Create command wrappers | ~5k per service | Medium |
| Modular files | Refactor large files | 5-10x on retries | High |
| Targeted skills | Load skills on-demand | ~3k per skill | Low |
| Agent model selection | Configure per-agent models | 3-18x per task | Medium |

**High-Impact Quick Wins:**
