## Hook Lifecycle and Integration

Plankton integrates with Claude Code's hook system through three hook types: PreToolUse (config protection), PostToolUse (quality enforcement), and Stop (config verification).

### Hook Execution Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant MainAgent as "Main Claude Agent"
    participant PreHook as "PreToolUse Hook<br/>protect_linter_configs.sh"
    participant EditTool as "Edit/Write Tool"
    participant PostHook as "PostToolUse Hook<br/>multi_linter.sh"
    participant Subprocess as "Claude Subprocess<br/>(tiered model)"
    participant StopHook as "Stop Hook<br/>stop_config_guardian.sh"
    
    MainAgent->>PreHook: About to edit file
    PreHook->>PreHook: Check if file is protected config<br/>(.ruff.toml, biome.json, etc.)
    
    alt Config file edit
        PreHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 2 (BLOCK)<br/>[hook] Config modification blocked
    else Non-config file
        PreHook-->>EditTool: Exit 0 (ALLOW)
        EditTool->>EditTool: Perform edit
        EditTool->>PostHook: Edit complete
        
        PostHook->>PostHook: Phase 1: Auto-format<br/>(silent, 40-50% fixed)
        PostHook->>PostHook: Phase 2: Collect violations<br/>(JSON structure)
        
        alt No violations
            PostHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 0 (no output)
        else Violations found
            PostHook->>Subprocess: Spawn claude -p with JSON<br/>(model tier based on codes)
            Subprocess->>Subprocess: Attempt fixes
            Subprocess-->>PostHook: Fixes applied
            PostHook->>PostHook: Re-run Phase 1 + 2<br/>(verify fixes)
            
            alt All fixed
                PostHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 0 (no output)
            else Violations remain
                PostHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 2<br/>[hook] N violation(s) remain
            end
        end
    end
    
    MainAgent->>MainAgent: Session ends
    MainAgent->>StopHook: Session end event
    StopHook->>StopHook: git diff --name-only<br/>(check for config changes)
    
    alt Config modified
        StopHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 0<br/>[hook] WARNING: Config file modified
    else No config changes
        StopHook-->>MainAgent: Exit 0 (silent)
    end
```

**Sources:** [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:19-55](), [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:57-66]()

### Main Agent Visibility Matrix

| Scenario | Agent Sees | Hook Exit Code | Hook Output |
|----------|-----------|----------------|-------------|
| No violations after Phase 1 | Nothing | 0 | (silent) |
| All violations fixed by subprocess | Nothing | 0 | (silent) |
| Violations remain after subprocess | Error message | 2 | `[hook] N violation(s) remain: {details}` |
| Advisory message (duplicates, old tooling) | Warning | 0 | `[hook:advisory] {message}` |
| Config edit blocked (PreToolUse) | Error message | 2 | `[hook] Config modification blocked` |
| Config modified detected (Stop) | Warning | 0 | `[hook] WARNING: Config file modified` |

**Sources:** [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:45-55]()

---

## Config Protection Architecture

Plankton implements three layers of defense against LLM attempts to modify linter configurations instead of fixing code violations.

### Protection Layers

**Diagram: Config Protection System**

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph Layer1["Layer 1: PreToolUse Block"]
        AgentEdit["Agent attempts Edit/Write"]
        CheckProtected{"File in protected list?"}
        ProtectedList["Protected Files:<br/>.ruff.toml<br/>biome.json<br/>.shellcheckrc<br/>.yamllint<br/>.hadolint.yaml<br/>etc."]
        BlockEdit["Exit 2: BLOCK<br/>[hook] Config modification blocked"]
        AllowEdit["Exit 0: ALLOW"]
    end
    
    subgraph Layer2["Layer 2: Stop Hook Detection"]
        SessionEnd["Session ends"]
        GitDiff["git diff --name-only HEAD"]
        CheckDiff{"Config files<br/>in diff?"}
        WarnUser["Exit 0<br/>[hook] WARNING: Config file modified"]
        Silent["Exit 0 (silent)"]
    end
    
    subgraph Layer3["Layer 3: File Integrity"]
        GitHistory["Git history"]
        CommitHook["Pre-commit hook<br/>(optional)"]
        RejectCommit["Reject commit with config changes"]
    end
    
    AgentEdit --> CheckProtected
    CheckProtected -->|"Yes"| ProtectedList
    ProtectedList --> BlockEdit
    CheckProtected -->|"No"| AllowEdit
    
    SessionEnd --> GitDiff
    GitDiff --> CheckDiff
    CheckDiff -->|"Yes"| WarnUser
    CheckDiff -->|"No"| Silent
    
    GitHistory --> CommitHook
    CommitHook --> RejectCommit
```

**Sources:** [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:57-66]()

### Protected File List

The following configuration files are protected from agent modification:

| Language/Tool | Protected Files |
|---------------|-----------------|
| **Python** | `.ruff.toml`, `ruff.toml`, `pyproject.toml` (ruff section) |
| **TypeScript/JS** | `biome.json`, `.eslintrc.*`, `tsconfig.json` |
| **Shell** | `.shellcheckrc`, `shellcheckrc` |
| **YAML** | `.yamllint`, `.yamllint.yaml` |
| **Dockerfile** | `.hadolint.yaml`, `hadolint.yaml` |
| **General** | `.editorconfig`, `.prettierrc.*` |

**Sources:** [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:57-66]()

### Package Manager Enforcement

Plankton also blocks legacy package managers via PreToolUse hook on Bash tool:

| Blocked Command | Replacement | Exceptions |
|-----------------|-------------|------------|
| `pip`, `pip3`, `poetry`, `pipenv` | `uv` | (none) |
| `npm`, `yarn`, `pnpm` | `bun` | `npm audit`, `npm view`, `npm publish` |

**Sources:** [skills/plankton-code-quality/SKILL.md:66-70]()

