## Custom Tools Integration

OpenCode provides native support for custom tools, which can be registered directly in `opencode.json` without requiring hook-based implementations. ECC provides three native custom tools:

### Tool: `run-tests`

**Purpose**: Execute test suite with framework detection

**Implementation**: `.opencode/tools/run-tests.js`

```javascript
// Pseudo-structure (not actual code)
{
  name: "run-tests",
  description: "Run project test suite with automatic framework detection",
  parameters: {
    pattern: { type: "string", optional: true },
    watch: { type: "boolean", optional: true }
  },
  handler: async (params) => {
    // Detect test framework (jest, vitest, pytest, go test)
    // Execute tests with appropriate command
    // Parse and format output
    // Return structured results
  }
}
```

### Tool: `check-coverage`

**Purpose**: Analyze test coverage and generate reports

**Implementation**: `.opencode/tools/check-coverage.js`

```javascript
// Pseudo-structure (not actual code)
{
  name: "check-coverage",
  description: "Analyze test coverage and identify uncovered code",
  parameters: {
    threshold: { type: "number", optional: true, default: 80 }
  },
  handler: async (params) => {
    // Execute coverage tool (nyc, coverage.py, go test -cover)
    // Parse coverage reports
    // Identify gaps and high-risk areas
    // Return structured coverage data
  }
}
```

### Tool: `security-audit`

**Purpose**: Scan codebase for security vulnerabilities

**Implementation**: `.opencode/tools/security-audit.js`

```javascript
// Pseudo-structure (not actual code)
{
  name: "security-audit",
  description: "Scan configuration and code for security issues",
  parameters: {
    severity: { type: "string", optional: true, default: "medium" }
  },
  handler: async (params) => {
    // Run dependency audit (npm audit, pip-audit, go list)
    // Check for hardcoded secrets
    // Validate MCP configurations
    // Analyze agent/hook permissions
    // Return security findings
  }
}
```

### Custom Tools vs Hook-Based Approach

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Claude Code Approach"
        CCHook["PostToolUse Hook"]
        CCBash["Bash Script"]
        CCParse["Manual Parsing"]
    end
    
    subgraph "OpenCode Approach"
        OCTool["Native Custom Tool"]
        OCHandler["JavaScript Handler"]
        OCStruct["Structured Output"]
    end
    
    CCHook --> CCBash
    CCBash --> CCParse
    
    OCTool --> OCHandler
    OCHandler --> OCStruct
    
    style OCStruct fill:#90EE90
```

**Advantages of Native Tools**:
- Structured input/output with type validation
- First-class integration with OpenCode UI
- Better error handling and timeout management
- Access to OpenCode internal APIs
- No shell escaping or parsing complexity

**Sources**: [docs/ja-JP/README.md:642-667](), [README.zh-CN.md:87-89]()

