# Page: Codex CLI Integration

# Codex CLI Integration

<details>
<summary>Relevant source files</summary>

The following files were used as context for generating this wiki page:

- [README.zh-CN.md](README.zh-CN.md)
- [agents/chief-of-staff.md](agents/chief-of-staff.md)
- [docs/ja-JP/README.md](docs/ja-JP/README.md)
- [docs/zh-TW/README.md](docs/zh-TW/README.md)

</details>



This page documents the integration of Everything Claude Code with Codex CLI, covering configuration structure, component distribution, and platform-specific limitations. Codex provides a sandbox permissions model and TOML-based configuration but does not yet support hook-based automation.

For native Claude Code integration with full hook support, see [Claude Code Integration](#12.1). For other platform integrations, see [Cursor IDE Integration](#12.2) and [OpenCode Integration](#12.3). For a feature comparison across all platforms, see [Platform Feature Comparison](#12.5).

---

## Overview

Codex CLI represents a minimal integration surface focused on agent definitions and skill distribution. Unlike Claude Code's native plugin system or OpenCode's rich event model, Codex prioritizes sandboxed execution with explicit permission grants and configuration-as-code via TOML files.

### Key Characteristics

| Aspect | Implementation |
|--------|----------------|
| **Configuration Format** | TOML (`.codex/config.toml`) |
| **Agent Distribution** | `AGENTS.md` supplement files |
| **Skill Distribution** | `.agents/skills/` directory structure |
| **Hook Support** | ❌ Not yet available |
| **Permission Model** | ✅ Sandbox with explicit grants |
| **Rules Support** | Manual integration required |
| **MCP Integration** | Standard MCP protocol support |

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## Architecture Overview

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "ECC Source"
        Core["Core Components<br/>agents/, skills/, commands/"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Codex Platform"
        Config[".codex/config.toml<br/>TOML configuration"]
        AgentSupp["AGENTS.md<br/>Agent supplement files"]
        SkillDir[".agents/skills/<br/>Skill definitions"]
        SandboxPerms["Sandbox Permissions<br/>Explicit grants"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Limitations"
        NoHooks["❌ No hooks yet<br/>Alternative: manual validation"]
        NoRules["❌ No native rules<br/>Alternative: custom prompts"]
        PartialCmds["⚠️ Partial commands<br/>Core workflow commands only"]
    end
    
    Core --> Config
    Core --> AgentSupp
    Core --> SkillDir
    
    Config --> SandboxPerms
    
    SkillDir -.limitation.-> NoHooks
    AgentSupp -.limitation.-> NoRules
    Config -.limitation.-> PartialCmds
    
    style NoHooks fill:#ffcccc
    style NoRules fill:#ffcccc
    style PartialCmds fill:#fff4cc
```

**Diagram: Codex Integration Architecture**

The integration adapts ECC components to Codex's TOML-based configuration and sandboxed execution model. Unlike Claude Code's hooks system or OpenCode's event-driven architecture, Codex requires explicit permission grants and lacks automated quality enforcement hooks.

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## Configuration Structure

### .codex/config.toml

Codex uses TOML for configuration rather than JSON. The configuration file defines agent metadata, model preferences, and permission grants.

**Example Structure**:

```toml
[agent.planner]
description = "Feature implementation planner"
model = "claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219"
temperature = 0.7

[agent.planner.permissions]
filesystem_read = true
filesystem_write = false
bash_execute = false

[agent.code-reviewer]
description = "Code quality and security reviewer"
model = "claude-opus-4-20250514"
temperature = 0.3

[agent.code-reviewer.permissions]
filesystem_read = true
filesystem_write = false
bash_execute = true
```

### Permission Model

Codex enforces a capability-based security model where each agent declares required permissions:

| Permission | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| `filesystem_read` | Read access to project files |
| `filesystem_write` | Write/edit file capabilities |
| `bash_execute` | Shell command execution |
| `network_access` | External API/network calls |
| `mcp_servers` | MCP server interaction |

Unlike Claude Code's open tool access, Codex requires explicit declaration preventing agents from performing undeclared operations.

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## Agent Distribution

### AGENTS.md Supplement Pattern

Codex uses supplemental `AGENTS.md` files rather than individual `.md` files per agent. This consolidated format reduces file system overhead.

```mermaid
graph LR
    ECC["ECC agents/<br/>14 individual .md files"]
    Transform["Translation Layer"]
    Codex["AGENTS.md<br/>Consolidated format"]
    
    ECC --> Transform
    Transform --> Codex
    
    subgraph "AGENTS.md Structure"
        Header["# Agent Definitions"]
        Planner["## planner<br/>YAML frontmatter + prompt"]
        Reviewer["## code-reviewer<br/>YAML frontmatter + prompt"]
        TDD["## tdd-guide<br/>YAML frontmatter + prompt"]
    end
    
    Codex --> Header
    Header --> Planner
    Planner --> Reviewer
    Reviewer --> TDD
```

**Diagram: Agent Distribution to AGENTS.md**

**Example AGENTS.md Format**:

```markdown
# Agent Definitions

## planner

```yaml
name: planner
description: Creates detailed implementation plans
model: claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob"]
```

You are a technical planner specializing in breaking down features...

---

## code-reviewer

```yaml
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for quality and security
model: claude-opus-4-20250514
tools: ["Read", "Grep", "Glob", "Bash"]
```

You are a senior code reviewer...
```

This format maintains backward compatibility with ECC's agent structure while adapting to Codex's single-file constraint.

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## Skills Distribution

### .agents/skills/ Directory Structure

Skills are distributed via the `.agents/skills/` directory, maintaining ECC's skill hierarchy:

```
.agents/
└── skills/
    ├── coding-standards/
    │   └── SKILL.md
    ├── tdd-workflow/
    │   └── SKILL.md
    ├── security-review/
    │   └── SKILL.md
    ├── verification-loop/
    │   └── SKILL.md
    └── continuous-learning-v2/
        └── SKILL.md
```

### Skill Loading Mechanism

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Agent as Codex Agent
    participant Loader as Skill Loader
    participant FS as .agents/skills/
    participant Prompt as Prompt Builder
    
    Agent->>Loader: Invoke skill "tdd-workflow"
    Loader->>FS: Read .agents/skills/tdd-workflow/SKILL.md
    FS-->>Loader: Markdown content + YAML frontmatter
    Loader->>Prompt: Inject skill into context
    Prompt-->>Agent: Skill-augmented prompt
    
    Note over Agent: Agent executes with<br/>skill-specific guidance
```

**Diagram: Skill Loading Sequence**

Skills are loaded on-demand when agents reference them, avoiding the context bloat from pre-loading all skills.

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## No Hooks Support

### Limitation Impact

Codex does not currently support hook-based automation. This affects several ECC features:

| ECC Hook | Claude Code | Codex Alternative |
|----------|-------------|-------------------|
| `PreToolUse` validation | ✅ Blocking validation | ❌ Manual verification |
| `PostToolUse` formatting | ✅ Auto-format on edit | ❌ Manual `/format` command |
| `PostToolUse` type checking | ✅ Automatic `tsc` | ❌ Manual `/verify` command |
| `SessionStart` context loading | ✅ Automatic resume | ❌ Manual context injection |
| `SessionEnd` persistence | ✅ Auto-save summary | ❌ Manual session notes |
| `Stop` pattern extraction | ✅ Continuous learning | ❌ Manual `/learn` invocation |

### Workaround Strategies

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Claude Code (Automated)"
        Edit1["Edit Tool"]
        PostHook1["PostToolUse Hook"]
        Format1["Auto-format (biome)"]
        Type1["Auto-typecheck (tsc)"]
        
        Edit1 --> PostHook1
        PostHook1 --> Format1
        PostHook1 --> Type1
    end
    
    subgraph "Codex (Manual)"
        Edit2["Edit Tool"]
        Manual["Manual Verification"]
        VerifyCmd["/verify command"]
        Format2["npx biome format"]
        Type2["npx tsc --noEmit"]
        
        Edit2 --> Manual
        Manual --> VerifyCmd
        VerifyCmd --> Format2
        VerifyCmd --> Type2
    end
    
    style PostHook1 fill:#90EE90
    style Manual fill:#ffcccc
```

**Diagram: Hook Workarounds in Codex**

Users must manually invoke verification commands that would otherwise run automatically via hooks.

**Recommended Manual Workflow**:

1. **After edits**: Run `/verify` to check build/types/lint
2. **Before commits**: Run `/checkpoint create` to validate state
3. **Session end**: Run `/sessions save` to persist context
4. **Pattern learning**: Run `/learn` to extract instincts

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 2 (Hook Lifecycle and Event Flow), High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

---

## Command Support

### Available Commands

Codex supports a subset of ECC commands focused on core workflows:

| Category | Commands | Status |
|----------|----------|--------|
| **Planning** | `/plan` | ✅ Full support |
| **Development** | `/tdd`, `/build-fix` | ✅ Full support |
| **Quality** | `/code-review`, `/verify`, `/checkpoint` | ✅ Full support |
| **Learning** | `/instinct-status`, `/instinct-import`, `/instinct-export`, `/evolve` | ✅ Full support |
| **Orchestration** | `/multi-plan`, `/multi-execute` | ⚠️ Limited (no PM2) |
| **Hooks** | N/A | ❌ Not applicable |

### Unsupported Features

Commands relying on hook infrastructure or external process management are not available:

- **PM2 commands** (`/pm2`): No process manager integration
- **Real-time observation** (`observe.sh`): No hook triggers
- **Auto-compaction** (`suggest-compact.js`): No tool call tracking
- **Session hooks** (`session-start.js`, `session-end.js`): No lifecycle events

**Sources**: High-level Diagram 4 (Cross-Platform Distribution Architecture)

